Yes, for quite some time.\" [2736] Charlotte Blacklock sat down at the table and burst into tears. [2737] \"You shouldn't have done that,\" she said. \"You shouldn't have imitated Dora's voice. I love Dora. I really do love Dora.\" [2738] Inspector Edward and the others crowded around the door. [2739] Sergeant Edwards, with his varied skills and knowledge of first aid and artificial respiration, was busy with Midge, who, as soon as she could speak, began to gush self-congratulatory words. [2740] \"I did splendidly, didn't I? I'm so clever! And I'm so brave! Oh, I'm so brave! Brave enough to be almost killed. But I dared to risk my life.\" [2741] Miss Quincannon pushed past the people around her, leaping towards Charlotte Blacklock, who was sobbing at the table. [2742] Chief Inspector Fletcher had to use all his strength to pull her back. [2743] \"Enough,\" he said, \"enough\u2014no, no, Miss Quincannon\u2014\"\n[2744] Miss Quincannon spoke through gritted teeth, \"Let me go and finish her off! Don't stop me. It was her who killed Amy Muggleton.\" [2745] Charlotte Blacklock looked up and snorted. [2746] \"I didn't want to kill her. I didn't want to kill anyone\u2014I had to, but I care about Dora. I was all alone after she died, ever since she died. Oh, Dora, Dora\u2014\"\n[2747] She bowed her head and covered her face with her hands, sobbing. [2748] Chapter XXIII: The Parsonage\n[2749] Miss Marple sat in a high-backed armchair. Round sat on the floor in front of the fireplace, hugging her knees. [2750] Reverend Julian leaned forward, not looking like a mature man but like a schoolboy. Inspector Corcoran puffed on his pipe and sipped his whisky and soda, obviously relieved of his burden and at ease. Seated around them were Julia, Patrick, Edmund, and Felicia. [2751] \"I think it's your turn to tell the story, Miss Maple,\" Corcoran said. [2752] \"Oh, no, my dear child. I just chipped in a little bit here and there. You were the one in charge, you orchestrated everything, and you knew so much more than I did.\" [2753] \"Well, let's all chip in then,\" Round said impatiently. \"One person at a time. Let's start with Auntie Jan, because I like the way her brain works. When did you start to suspect that it was all a setup by Blacklock?\" [2754] \"Well, my dear Round, it's hard to say. Of course, from the beginning, it seemed like the ideal person to arrange the robbery, or at least the most conspicuous one, was Miss Blacklock herself. She was the only known person to have had contact with Rudi Scherz, and it would have been so easy to plan something like this in her own home. For example, turning off the central heating would have meant no need for a fire, which would have meant no light in the house. And only the house's owner could have made such an arrangement. [2755] \"I didn't always think this way - it's a shame things weren't simpler! Oh, no, I was fooled like everyone else, because I thought someone really wanted to kill Letitia Blacklock.\" [2756] \"I'd rather get to the bottom of what really happened,\" Round said. \"Did the Swiss boy recognize her?\" [2757] \"Yes. He worked at \u2013\"\n[2758] She hesitated, looking at Coraquil. [2759] \"In Bern, at Dr. Adolf Koch's clinic,\" Coraquil said. \"Koch was a world-renowned expert in thyroid surgery. Charlotte Blacklock went there to have her thyroid removed, and Rudi Scherz was a janitor. He recognized her when he came to England, and he struck up a conversation with her. I dare say, if he had thought about it, he wouldn't have done it, because he was sent away from home for misbehavior. However, it was some time after Charlotte left there, so she wouldn't have known about it.\" [2760] \"So, he didn't mention Monte Carlo and his father being the hotel owners?\" [2761] \"Ah, no, that was something she had to fabricate to explain why he spoke to her.\" [2762] \"Seeing him must have been a shock to her,\" said Miss Marple thoughtfully. \"She was safe, and then, due to an almost impossible coincidence, someone she knew appeared, not as one of the two Miss Blacklocks\u2014which she was prepared for\u2014but as Charlotte Blacklock, the patient who had had the thyroid operation. She was recognized, not as a guest at the hotel, but as the patient.\" [2763] \"Now, let me tell you from the beginning. I think\u2014if Inspector Kollarik agrees with me\u2014that it was Charlotte Blacklock, a beautiful, carefree, and loving girl who developed a thyroid enlargement. The illness ruined her life, as she was a sensitive girl and one who placed great importance on her appearance. And girls at that age are particularly sensitive about themselves. If she had a mother or a father who was understanding, I don't think she would have fallen into that state of mind, but unfortunately, she was suffering greatly. She had no one to take her out of her shell, to force her to socialize, and to make her lead a normal life, instead of fixating on her deformity. Of course, in another family, she might have had the surgery years ago. [2764] \"However, I think Dr. Blacklock was a conservative man, narrow-minded, cruel, and stubborn. He didn't believe in this surgery. Charlotte would have gotten the conclusion that she was powerless\u2014except for iodine and other drugs. Charlotte did believe him, and I think her sister trusted him too much as a physician. [2765] \"Charlotte expressed her loyalty to her father in a fragile and melancholic way, and she probably thought he was the most correct. She withdrew further into herself, and as a result, her thyroid continued to grow larger, and she became increasingly reclusive, refusing to see people. However, she was a kind and loving person at heart.\" [2766] \"It's strange to describe a murderer in that way,\" said Edmund. [2767] \"I don't agree,\" said Miss Marple. \"Weak and kind-hearted people are often the ones who betray most easily. Once they have a grudge against life, their moral strength is quickly drained away by it. [2768] \"Lettice Blacklock's character, however, was very different. Inspector Corcoran told me that Bell Goddard described her as too good, and I agree that Lettice was good. She was a moral person \u2013 in her own words \u2013 she couldn't understand why others couldn't see the dishonesty. She would never have the slightest thought of doing anything dishonest, no matter what the temptation. Lettice was very loyal to her sister. She wrote to her, telling her about everything that happened, trying to keep her in touch with life. She was very worried about Charlotte's mental state. [2769] \"Finally, Dr. Blacklock died. Lettice gave up her position at Randall Goddard's without hesitation and devoted her life to Charlotte. She took her to Switzerland to consult with authorities about the possibility of surgery. The surgery was too late, but we know it was successful. The deformity was removed, and the scars left by the operation were easily concealed with a string of pearls or a necklace. [2770] \"The end.\" [2771] \"Later, the war broke out, and the sisters found it difficult to return to England, so they stayed in Switzerland and worked for the Red Cross and other organizations. Is that correct, Inspector?\" [2772] \"Yes, Miss Marple.\" [2773] \"They occasionally heard news from England. I believe they also heard that Bell Goodale was not long for this world. I believe, purely out of human nature, they planned and discussed how they would spend the money after it was theirs. I think it must be recognized that for the sisters, this prospect meant more to Charlotte. For the first time in her life, Charlotte could feel like a normal woman and go anywhere without fear of being met with disgust or pity. She could finally enjoy life to the fullest, making up for lost time. She wanted to travel, buy a house and a beautiful garden, wear pretty clothes and sparkling jewelry, go to the theater and concert halls, and indulge every whim. For Charlotte, it was like a fairy tale coming true. [2774] \"However, Lady Lestrange, who was in good health, contracted the flu, which turned into pneumonia, and she died within a week. Charlotte not only lost her sister but also her dream. I think she almost resented Lady Lestrange for dying. They had just received a letter saying that Bell Goodale was not long for this world. At such a critical moment, why did Lady Lestrange have to die? Perhaps in a month, the money would have been Lady Lestrange's \u2013 once she died, it would have been hers\u2026\n[2775] \"At this point, I think the difference between the two became apparent. Charlotte did not feel that her thoughts were wrong \u2013 she thought there was nothing wrong with them. The money was meant for Lady Lestrange \u2013 it would have been hers in a few months \u2013 she saw herself and Lady Lestrange as the same person.\" [2776] \"Perhaps it was when someone asked her sister's Christian name that she had the idea. Suddenly, she realized that in the eyes of most people, the two Miss Blacklocks were indistinguishable - elderly, well-bred British women, dressed almost identically, with faces so similar that they were virtually identical. (I had pointed out to Roundell that older women look alike.) Why couldn't it have been Charlotte who died and Leticia who lived? [2777] \"I think it was more a matter of impulse than careful planning. Leticia was buried under Charlotte's name. 'Charlotte' died, and 'Leticia' returned to England. The creativity and energy that nature had given her had been dormant for years and now came to the fore. As Charlotte, she had been a minor character. Now she had a face that dominated others - the sense of dominance that belonged to Leticia. Their brains were not very different, I believe, but their moral differences were vast. [2778] \"Charlotte naturally took a few obvious measures. She bought a house in an unfamiliar place in England. She had to avoid only a few people from her hometown - she had lived a solitary life there - and Bella Goddard, who was too familiar with Leticia to pull off the switch. Despite her arthritic fingers, she managed to forge the handwriting. It was actually quite easy, as few people really knew Charlotte.\u201d\n[2779] \"What if she met someone who knew Leticia?\" Roundell asked. \"There must have been quite a few.\" [2780] \"They would not be a problem either. Someone might say, 'I met Leticia the other day. She's changed so much that I didn't recognize her.' But their minds would not doubt that it was not Leticia. Ten years can change a person. And she could always blame her poor eyesight for not recognizing them. You must remember that she knew all the details of Leticia's life in London, including the people she knew and the places she went. She could refer to the letters Leticia had written to her, mention some events, or ask about the circumstances of mutual friends, and quickly dispel any doubts. No, she was only afraid of being recognized as Charlotte.\" [2781] \"She settled down in Little Fulham and got to know the neighbors. Later, she received a letter asking for dear Letitia's help, and she happily accepted the visit of two young cousins she had never met. [2782] \"Everything proceeded flawlessly. It was at this point that she made a grave mistake. This mistake stemmed entirely from her compassionate nature and benevolent disposition. She received a letter from an old school friend who was down on her luck, so she went to help her. Perhaps part of the reason was that, despite having everything, she was lonely. Her secret had made her shun others. She had always fondly remembered Dorothy Bunner as a symbol of her carefree and happy days at school. Regardless, in a moment of impulse, she wrote a letter to Dorothy herself. Dorothy would have been overjoyed: she wrote to Letitia, and the reply was from her sister Charlotte. It would have been impossible to pretend to be Letitia with Dorothy. Dorothy was one of the few people introduced to her by Charlotte during her lonely and melancholy days. [2783] \"Because she knew Dorothy would speak her mind, she told her what she had done. Dorothy wholeheartedly agreed. In her muddled mind, she believed that Letit should not have been deprived of her inheritance because of Charlotte's death. Since Letit had bravely endured all the suffering, she deserved recompense. It would have been unfair if the money had fallen into the hands of someone who had never heard of her. [2784] \"She knew that this must be kept secret. It was like an extra pound of butter that was fine, but couldn't be talked about. So, Dorothy came to Little Fulham. And soon, Charlotte realized that she had made a terrible mistake. Not only was it due to Dorothy's poor eyesight and clumsy hands, which caused many mistakes and drove people crazy when living with her, but also because Dorothy became a real crisis. Although Charlotte and Letitia called each other by their full names, Dorothy was always using nicknames. And although she had learned to call her friend Letit, the old name often slipped out of her mouth. In addition, memories of the past were easily brought up - Charlotte had to constantly keep her from accidentally blurting out something due to forgetfulness. This began to make her anxious. [2785] \"However, it was unlikely that anyone\n[2786] \"But she might have a different opinion. She might think it was a despicable blackmail, suspecting that he might have discovered something about her and that, once the news of Bella Goddle's death was published in the newspapers, he might realize that he had found a gold mine on her. [2787] \"Now she was determined to deceive. She had appeared as Letty Blacklock, using that identity both for the bank and for Mrs. Goddle. The only unexpected obstacle was the rather dubious Swiss hotel waiter, whose character was far from reliable and who might be a fraud. Once he was out of the way, she could rest easy. [2788] \"Perhaps she initially conceived this plan as a fantasy. She had experienced the cravings of love and drama in her life, so she delightedly elaborated on the details. So, how could she get rid of him? [2789] \"Now she was determined to deceive. She had appeared as Letty Blacklock, using that identity both for the bank and for Mrs. Goddle. The only unexpected obstacle was the rather dubious Swiss hotel waiter, whose character was far from reliable and who might be a fraud. Once he was out of the way, she could rest easy. [2790] \"Perhaps she initially conceived this plan as a fantasy. She had experienced the cravings of love and drama in her life, so she delightedly elaborated on the details. So, how could she get rid of him?\" [2791] \"She formulated her plan and ultimately decided to put it into action. She told Rudi Scherz the story of playing a robbery game at the party, explaining that a stranger would play the role of the 'thief,' and promising him a large sum of money for his cooperation.\" [2792] \"He agreed without hesitation, which further convinces me that Scherz did not have any leverage over her. To him, she was just a foolish old lady willing to part with her money.\" [2793] \"She gave him the ad to place in the newspaper, arranged for him to visit the small estate to study the layout of the grounds, and showed him the meeting place - where she would pick him up on the night of the murder and lead him into the house. Of course, Miss Bunner had no idea about any of this.\" [2794] \"Then, the day came -\" he paused. [2795] Miss Marple continued in her gentle voice. [2796] \"She must have been very nervous that day. You see, it was not too late to turn back... Dora Bunner told us that Letitia was very frightened that day, and of course she was. She was afraid of what she was about to do, afraid that the plan would go wrong, but not afraid enough to turn back.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2797] \"Perhaps, stealing the revolver from Colonel Eastbrook's drawer was fun. Slipping up to the empty room upstairs while talking about eggs and jam. Lubricating the second door so that it opened and closed smoothly and silently - that was fun too. Moving the table outside the door so that Phyllida's arrangement of flowers would stand out more prominently - that was interesting. It was all like a game, but what was to follow was no longer a game. Yes, she was very frightened... Dora Bunner was not wrong.\" [2798] \"After all, she carried out the plan, and everything went according to plan. At 6:30, she went out to 'round up the ducks', letting Scherz in, giving him a mask, cape, gloves, and a flashlight. By the time the clock struck six, everything was ready, and she stood at the table near the arcade, reaching for the cigarette box on the table. She did it so naturally. The host, Patrick, went to get the drink. And she - the hostess - was reaching for the cigarettes. She correctly deduced that everyone would look at the clock when it struck six. And that's exactly what happened. Only one person, loyal Dora, kept her eyes on her friend. When she was first questioned, she accurately described what Miss Blacklock was doing at the time, saying that Miss Blacklock picked up the vase of violets.\" [2799] \"She had previously damaged the lamp's wiring, leaving the copper wire almost exposed. The whole process took only a second. The cigarette box, the vase, and the small switch were all within reach. She picked up the vase, splashed water on the exposed wire, and turned on the lamp switch. Water is an excellent conductor of electricity, and the fuse blew.\" [2800] \"Just like that afternoon here,\" said Round. \"It really gave you a fright, didn't it, Aunt Jane?\" [2801] \"Yes, my dear. I've been worried about the lamp. I realized there were two table lamps, a pair, and one had been switched for the other - probably done at night.\" [2802] \"That's right,\" said Cora Darcy. \"The next morning, Fletcher checked the table lamps and found that they were as undamaged as the other lamps, with no damage to the wires or melting.\" [2803] \"I understand what Dora Bunner meant by saying she was a shepherdess the day before,\" said Miss Marple. \"But I followed her thinking and fell into this thinking error, thinking it was Patrick who did it. One interesting thing about Dora Bunner is that when she repeats what she hears, she is not reliable, she always exaggerates or distorts facts with her imagination, and her imagination is often wrong; but when she sees something, she describes it very accurately. She saw Letitia pick up the vase of violets -\"\n[2804] \"And she also saw what she described as a flash and a crackle,\" Cora Darcy interjected. [2805] \"Of course, dear Round, when you spilled water from the vase of Christmas roses on the table lamp wires, I immediately realized that only Miss Blacklock herself could have burned the lamp, because only she was closest to the table.\" [2806] \"I should have kicked myself,\" said Coraldrake. \"Miss Bunner even mentioned the table being scarred from heat because someone 'put a cigarette on it,' but there was no one smoking, and the violets died for lack of water\u2014a mistake by Letitia, who should have refilled the vase. But I suppose she thought no one would notice, and Miss Bunna easily convinced herself she hadn't refilled it to begin with.\" [2807] He went on. [2808] \"Certainly, she's easily influenced, and Miss Blacklock has taken advantage of that more than once. I believe Miss Bunna's suspicions of Patrick were also induced. \" [2809] \"Why pick on me?\" Patrick asked in a hurt tone. [2810] \"I don't think it was a deliberate suggestion, but it prevented Miss Bunna from suspecting Miss Blacklock as the mastermind behind this tragedy. Well, as we know, when the lights went out, everyone started screaming, and she slipped out the door she had previously oiled, coming up behind Mr. Rudy Scherz, who was playing with a flashlight, eagerly acting out his role. I don't think he realized she was behind him, wearing gardening gloves and holding a revolver. She waited for the flashlight to shine on the wall she needed to aim at, and then fired two shots. When he turned around in surprise, she pointed the gun at him and fired again. She dropped the revolver next to his body, then casually tossed the gloves onto the table in the hallway and returned to her place, where she had been standing before the lights went out. She cut herself on the ear, and I'm not quite sure how\u2014\"\n[2811] \"I think she used a fingernail file,\" said Miss Maple, \"just a nick on the earlobe would make a lot of blood. Of course, it's a good psychological tactic. The blood on her white dress made it look like she had been shot and almost died.\" [2812] \"Everything was going well,\" said Corrado, \"Dora Bonner insisted that Scherz had definitely shot Miss Blacklock, and that worked. Although not intentionally, Dora Bonner conveyed the impression that she had actually seen her friend injured. The case could have been closed with suicide or accidental death. And it was thanks to Miss Maple here that it wasn't.\" [2813] \"Ah, no, no,\" Miss Maple shook her head vigorously. \"All my modest efforts were purely coincidental. It was you, Inspector Corrado, who was dissatisfied with the conclusion. It was you who prevented the case from being closed.\" [2814] \"I was not entirely satisfied with the conclusion,\" said Corrado. \"I knew something was wrong, but I couldn't see where it was until you showed me the way. After that, Miss Blacklock was truly in trouble. I discovered that the second door had been tampered with. We had all assumed that what had happened was just one possibility, but we had no real evidence except for our deductions. The oiled door was the evidence. It was a lucky guess, and purely coincidental - I pulled the wrong handle.\" [2815] \"I think you were guided there, Inspector,\" said Miss Maple. \"But, to be fair, I'm getting old.\" [2816] \"The search resumed,\" Corcoran said, \"but this time it was different. We were now looking for someone with a motive to kill Letitia Blacklock.\" [2817] \"And there was someone with a motive,\" Miss Marple said, \"Miss Blacklock knew it. I think she recognized Philippa almost at first sight. Because among those allowed to enter Charlotte's private life, Sonia Goddard was one of the few. And as people get older - something you don't know, Inspector Corcoran - they remember faces they saw when they were young better than those they saw a year or two ago. Philippa was probably about the same age as Sonia when she was young, and she looked very much like her mother. It's strange, but I think that after recognizing Philippa, Charlotte was actually happy. She liked Philippa and wanted to treat her like a daughter. She thought she would take care of Philippa when she inherited the money, and she would treat her like a daughter. Philippa and Harry should live with her. She was happy to do a good deed. But once the inspector started asking questions and found a pair of 'Pip and Emma,' Charlotte became restless. She didn't want Philippa to be a scapegoat, and her whole plan was to make it look like a young criminal came to rob, and the criminal died in an accident. But then, with the oiled door being discovered, the whole plan changed. Besides Philippa - whom I know for sure doesn't know Julia's true identity - no one else could have had a motive to kill her. She made every effort to hide Philippa's true identity. When you asked her, she thought quickly and told you that Sonia was short and had dark skin, and then she took Letitia's photo and also took Sonia's photo from the album, so you couldn't notice any similarities between Philippa and Sonia.\" [2818] \"And to make me suspect Swetnam as Sonia,\" Corcoran said with disgust. [2819] \"My poor mother,\" Edmund whispered, \"a woman with an impeccable life, or so I always believed.\" [2820] \"But,\" Miss Marple continued, \"the real danger was Dora Bunner. Dora was getting more and more forgetful and talkative every day. I remember how Charlotte looked at her that day when we had tea. Do you know why? Dora called her Lois. We thought it was a slip of the tongue, but it scared Charlotte. So everything continued. Poor Dora went on talking. That day we had coffee at 'Bluebird,' and I had a very strange impression that Dora was talking about two people, not one, but of course she was talking about the same person. She would say her friend wasn't pretty but had character, and then describe her as a pretty and carefree girl. She talked about Letit's cleverness and success, but then said how sad her life was, quoting a poem about 'bravely bearing the pain.' But this didn't seem to fit Letitia's life. I think that when Charlotte walked into the coffee shop\n[2821] \"It is probably the conversation in the coffee shop that truly sealed Dora's fate \u2013 if you allow for such an exaggeration. But I believe the outcome is the same... because as long as Dora Bonner is alive, Charlotte's life is not safe. She loves Dora and does not want to kill her, but she sees no other way out. And I anticipate \u2013 as I mentioned to you about the case of Nurse Ellerton \u2013 that she convinces herself that this is almost an act of mercy. Poor Bonnie \u2013 she won't live long anyway, and it may be a painful death. It is strange that she tries to make Bonnie's last day as happy as possible. Birthday dinner \u2013 a special cake...\"\n[2822] \"The Death of Tasty.\" Philippa shivered. [2823] \"Yes, yes, very much like that... She tried to make her friend happy until the end... a dinner with all her favorite foods, no one saying anything to upset her. Then, the pills in the aspirin bottle, whatever they were. She placed the pills on her nightstand, so when Bonnie couldn't find her new bottle, she would have to go to Letty's room to get some, making it seem like the pills were specifically for Letty...\n[2824] \"As a result, Bonnie passed away peacefully in her sleep, and Charlotte felt secure again. However, she missed Dora Bunner, missed her love and loyalty, missed talking about the past with Dora... she wept bitterly on the day I delivered the note to Julian, and her grief was sincere, for she had killed her beloved friend...\"\n[2825] \"This is too terrible,\" Yuan Yuan said, \"terrible.\" [2826] \"Yet it's human nature,\" said Julian Harmon, \"people often forget that murderers are very human too.\" [2827] \"I know,\" said Miss Marple, \"people, usually deserve pity, and are also extremely dangerous. Especially someone like Charlotte Blacklock, who is weak and good-hearted. This is because once a weak person is really afraid, they can become cruel and lose all self-control out of fear.\" [2828] \"And what about Muggleton?\" asked Julian. [2829] \"Yes, poor Miss Muggleton. Charlotte must have overheard them rehearsing the murder when she went to the cottage. The windows were open, and she just listened. Before that, she never thought there was anyone else who could be a threat. Miss Chippchase encouraged her friend to recall what she saw, but before that, Charlotte thought it was impossible for anyone to see the truth. She must have been listening at the window. Would it be a problem? Suddenly, just as Miss Chippchase rushed out to the police station, Miss Muggleton stumbled upon the truth. She called out behind her: 'She wasn't there\u2026'\u201d\n[2830] \"I asked Miss Chippchase about Miss Muggleton's way of saying that... because if she had said 'She wasn't there,' it would have been different.\" [2831] \"That's too subtle for me,\" said Cora Dedalus. [2832] Miss Maple's fair cheeks flushed, and she turned to him eagerly. [2833] \"Just imagine what Miss Muggleton was thinking... People often overlook things and don't realize them. There was once a railway accident, but I only remember a pool of paint on the side of the carriage, which I could still draw later. And once in London, a bomb fell from the sky, shattering glass everywhere, causing panic, but I remember most vividly a woman standing in front of me, with a hole in her stocking at the thigh, and the two stockings didn't match. So when Miss Muggleton stopped daydreaming and tried to recall what she had seen, she remembered a lot of things. She started recalling from the fireplace, with the flashlight shining first here, then following the light to the two windows, with someone in between her and the windows. For example, Mrs. Harmon had her hands over her eyes. Her mind followed the beam of light. Then her thoughts turned to the stunned Miss Bonner, an empty wall, a table with a lamp and a cigarette box, and then the gunshot \u2013 the most unbelievable thing in her memory. She saw the wall, and later there were two bullet holes in it, where Miss Blacklock leaned when she was shot, and Miss Letty was not there...\n[2834] \"Do you understand what I mean? Miss Chinchcliff asked her to recall where the three women were, and she focused on that. If one of them was not there, she could pinpoint that person and say, 'Ah, that's it! She wasn't there!' But what she remembered was the location \u2013 the place where someone should have been \u2013 but it was empty \u2013 no one was there. The place was still there, but the person was gone. She couldn't believe it. 'It's surprising, Chinch,' she said, 'she wasn't there...'\"\n[2835] \"I asked Miss Chinchcliff about the way Miss Muggleton said that phrase... because if she had said 'She wasn't there,' it would have meant something different.\" [2836] \"But you knew that before, didn't you?\" Round said, \"When the lamp burnt out, you wrote those things on the paper.\" [2837] \"Yes, my dear. All the clues came together, you see, all the fragmented and unrelated things formed a coherent pattern.\" [2838] Round quoted softly: \" 'Lamp? Yes. Violet? Yes. The bottle of aspirin.' You mean that on the day Bonnie bought a new bottle, she didn't need to take Letty's? \" [2839] \"Unless her own bottle was taken or hidden by someone. It looked like someone wanted to kill Letty Blacklock.\" [2840] \"Yes, I see. 'Death by Delight.' It was the cake, not just the cake. The whole dinner was a trap to make Bonnie happy for a day and then die. Treating her like a dog to be put down. What I find most terrifying is this - a false sense of compassion.\" [2841] \"She was a good woman. She was telling the truth in the kitchen: 'I don't want to kill anyone.' She craved money that didn't belong to her. This desire - it became an obsession - to use that money to compensate for all the suffering life had given her - had not been satisfied yet, and everything was lost. People who resent life are often dangerous, they seem to feel that life owes them too much. I know many disabled people who have suffered much more than Charlotte Blacklock and have been deprived of more by life. One's happiness and misery depend on oneself. But oh, dear, I digress, where were we?\" [2842] \"To your list,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"What did you mean by 'consult'?\" [2843] Miss Marple nodded mischievously at Inspector Corrigan. [2844] \"You must have seen this, Inspector Corrigan. You showed me the letter Letitia Blacklock wrote to her sister. The word 'consult' appeared twice, and each time it was spelled with an e. But in the note I had Yuan Yuan give you, Miss Blacklock wrote 'consult' with an i.\" [2845] \"Yes,\" agreed Inspector Corrigan. \"I should have noticed that.\" [2846] Round continued, \"Endure bravely the torture of pain.\" That's what Bonnie said to you in the caf\u00e9, Letitia certainly didn't suffer. And \"iodine\", did that clue lead you to think of thyroid enlargement? [2847] \"Yes, dear. You know, Switzerland, and Letitia Blacklock gave the impression that her 'sister' died of lung disease. But I remember that at the time, the most skilled and authoritative surgeon for thyroid enlargement was Swiss. This connects with Letitia Blacklock's peculiar necklace that she never took off. The jewelry wasn't in her usual style \u2013 it was perfect for concealing scars.\" [2848] \"Now I understand why she was so agitated when the necklace broke that night,\" said Colonel Corcoran. \"It seemed very unusual at the time.\" [2849] \"Later, you wrote 'Lotty' instead of the name we thought was Letitia,\" said Round. [2850] \"Yes, I remember that the sister's name was Charlotte. Dorothy Bunner once or twice called Miss Blacklock 'Lotty', and each time she did, she seemed uneasy.\" [2851] \"So what about Bern and the pension?\" [2852] \"Rudi worked as a janitor in a hospital in Bern.\" [2853] \"And the pension?\" [2854] \"Oh, my dear Yuan Yuan, I mentioned it to you at 'Bluebird', but only casually, without thinking about using it here. Mrs. Wutherspoon took Mrs. Butler's pension in addition to her own, but Mrs. Butler had been dead for years. Because the old ladies look so similar, yes, all this forms a pattern. I was so excited at the time that I went out to let my mind cool down and think about how to prove everything. Later, Miss Chinchcliffe picked me up, and we found Miss Muggleton...\"\n[2855] Miss Maple's tone lowered, and all the joy and excitement disappeared, leaving only calm. [2856] \"I knew I had to do something, and do it quickly. But there was no real evidence. So I came up with a feasible plan and told Chief Constable Fletcher.\" [2857] \"I gave Fletcher a good talking-to!\" said Colonel Draco. \"He had no right to agree to your plan without consulting me first.\" [2858] \"He didn't like it, but I convinced him,\" said Miss Marple. \"We went to the estate and found Micky.\" [2859] Julia drew in a sharp breath. \"I can't imagine how you managed to convince her.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHammers?\" [2114] \"I mean, what are you going to do? Tell everyone? Is that necessary? Fair? Conscience clear?\" [2115] \"Does no one know about this?\" [2116] \"No one here knows,\" her voice changed, \"my son, he doesn't know. I don't want him to know. I'll never let him know.\" [2117] \"So I have to say, Mrs. Hemes, you're taking a big risk. Tell him when he grows up and understands. But if one day he discovers the truth on his own, it won't be good for him. If you continue to tell him that his father is a heroic martyr\u2014\"\n[2118] \"I don't do that. I'm not completely dishonest, I just don't mention it. His father is dead. After all, that's all we know.\" [2119] \"But your husband is still alive?\" [2120] \"Maybe, how would I know?\" [2121] \"When was the last time you saw him, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2122] Phyllida answered quickly: \"I haven't seen him for years.\" [2123] \"You're sure about that? For example, you didn't see him two weeks ago?\" [2124] \"What are you implying?\" [2125] \"That you met with Rudi Scherz in the gazebo, I never thought that was likely. But Micky's story was so convincing. I believe, Mrs. Hammers, that the man you saw when you came back from work that morning was your husband.\" [2126] \"I didn't meet anyone in the gazebo.\" [2127] \"Maybe he was short of money and you helped him out?\" [2128] \"I told you I haven't seen him. I didn't meet anyone in the gazebo!\" [2129] \"Deserters are usually desperate men. You know, they often get involved in robberies, burglaries, and the like. And they bring back foreign-made revolvers from abroad.\" [2130] \"I don't know where my husband is. I haven't seen him for many years.\" [2131] \"Is that your final statement, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2132] \"I have nothing else to say.\" [2133] \"This is your final statement, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2134] \"I have nothing else to say.\" [2135] \"Stubborn as a mule.\" He muttered to himself, angry. He was sure that Mrs. Hammers was lying, but he couldn't break her stubborn denial. [2136] He wished he knew more about this former Captain Himmelman. The information he had was scant, with a tarnished military record, but that didn't mean Himmelman had fallen into criminality. [2136]\n[2137] After all, Himmelman had nothing to do with the oil on the door. [2137]\n[2138] It was someone in the house, or someone who could easily enter the house. [2138]\n[2139] He stood looking up the stairs, suddenly wondering what Julia was doing up in the attic. An attic, he mused, was not a place where the fastidious Julia would willingly venture. [2139]\n[2140] What was she doing up there? [2140]\n[2141] He creeps up the stairs, no one around, and pushes open the door that Julia had come out of, climbing the narrow staircase up to the attic. [2142] There are some large trunks, small trunks, and various broken household items, such as a chair missing a leg, a broken ceramic table lamp, and some antique cutlery. [2143] He turns to the large trunk and opens one of the lids. It's all clothes. Old-fashioned, of good quality, all women's wear. He assumes they belong to Miss Blacklock or her deceased sister. [2144] He opens another trunk. [2145] It's all curtains. [2146] He turned to a small briefcase, which contained some documents and letters. The letters were old and the paper was yellowed. [2147] He looked at the suitcase's exterior, which had the initials C.L.B. written on it. He correctly deduced that the suitcase belonged to Charlotte, Letitia's deceased sister. He opened one of the letters. The beginning read:\n[2148] Dearest Charlotte:\n[2149] Yesterday, Bella felt well enough to go on a picnic. Randall Goodale took a day off. The issue of Asworth stock was a great success. He was very pleased. The preferred stock exceeded par value. [2150] He skimmed the rest of the letter and looked at the signature:\n[2151] Yours lovingly, Letitia\n[2152] He picked up another letter. [2153] Dear Charlotte:\n[2154] I hope you occasionally feel like interacting with people. You know, you're exaggerating. Things aren't as bad as you think. Besides, people don't care about such things. It's not as bad as you imagine. [2155] He nodded. He remembered Bell Goodale saying that Charlotte Blacklock had suffered some kind of disfigurement or deformity. As a result, Letitia had resigned from her job to take care of her sister. These letters revealed her deep affection and concern for a disabled person. She wrote to her sister, detailing every event that happened around her and meticulously describing every detail that she thought might interest her sick sister. Charlotte had kept all these letters. Occasionally, there were also strange snapshots attached to the letters. [2156] Corradino's heart suddenly swelled with excitement. Perhaps he could find a clue here. The letters written by Letizia Blacklock herself may have long been forgotten. Here faithfully recreated a scene from the past, where there might still be a clue that could help him understand the unknown. [2157] The photos might as well \u2013 just possibly \u2013 contain a photo of Sonia Goodale, and the person who took the other photos of Sonia might not have known that. [2158] Inspector Corradino carefully rewrapped the letters and closed the box, then walked downstairs. [2159] Letizia Blacklock stood at the bottom of the staircase, staring at him in astonishment. \"Was it you up there? I heard footsteps, and I couldn't imagine who \u2013\"\n[2160] \"Miss Blacklock, I found some letters you wrote to your sister many years ago. May I take them back to look at them?\" [2161] She flushed with anger. [2162] \"Must you do this? What good will it do you?\" [2163] \"They might give me a picture of Sonia Goddard, her character \u2013 maybe there will be some clues that could help with the case \u2013 and the events.\" [2164] \"These are private letters, Inspector.\" [2165] \"I know.\" [2166] \"I guess you'll take them... I suppose you have the right to, anyway you could easily get hold of them. Take them \u2013 take them! But you won't find much about Sonia in them. She married and left me when I started working for Randall Goodale a year or two later.\" [2167] \"There might be something,\" Corrado insisted. \"We can't leave any stone unturned. I assure you, there is a danger to you.\" [2168] She bit her lip. \"I understand. Bonnie died \u2013 from aspirin meant for me. Next could be Patrick, or Julia, Philly, or Micky \u2013 any of the promising young people. Or the person who gave me the drink, or the one who gave me the chocolate. Oh! Take the letters \u2013 take them. Burn them after you've read them. They mean nothing to me and Charlotte now. The past is over \u2013 gone \u2013 done with. No one remembers \u2013\"\n[2169] She lifted her hand and touched the false pearl necklace she wore. Corrado thought it clashed with her tweed suit and skirt. [2170] She repeated, \"Take the letters.\" [2171] 3\n[2172] The next afternoon, the Inspector visited the parsonage. [2173] It was a dark, windy day. Miss Marple pulled her chair closer to the fire and knitted wool. Roundworm crawled around the floor, cutting fabric according to the pattern. [2174] Miss Marple leaned back and brushed a strand of hair out of her eyes, looking expectantly at Coradine. [2175] \"I don't know if this violates confidentiality,\" the Inspector said to Miss Marple, \"but I'd like you to see this letter.\" [2176]He explained the circumstances under which he had found these letters in the attic. [2177]\"They were quite moving letters,\" he said, \"written by Miss Blacklock to keep her sister interested in life, to keep her in good health. Behind these sisters, there was a very clear image of their old-fashioned father, Dr. Blacklock. A true stick-in-the-mud, a bully, utterly self-righteous, and convinced that everything he thought and did was right. Perhaps because of his stubbornness, he had killed hundreds of patients. He could not tolerate any new ideas or methods.\" [2178]\"I don't know if I should blame him for that,\" Miss Marple said. \"I always thought that young doctors were eager to try and rush things. After they had extracted all our teeth, filled our strange glands with drugs, and gradually removed our organs, they admitted that they were powerless. Honestly, I prefer the old-fashioned remedies, the ones in the black bottles. After all, you can just pour the medicine down the drain.\" [2179]She took the letter from Colonel Darcy. [2180]He spoke up: \"I asked you to look at this letter because I thought you would understand this generation better than I do. I really don't understand what's going on in their heads.\" [2181] Miss Marple opened the brittle paper. [2182] My dearest Charlotte:\n[2183] I haven't written to you for two days because we've had the most terrible family quarrel. Sonya, Randell's sister (do you remember her? She picked you up in her car that day. I wish you would go out more often.) Sonya announced that she was going to marry a man named Dmitri Stanforth. I've only met him once. He's very attractive, but I must say, he's not to be relied upon. Randell strongly opposed him, calling him a scoundrel and a fraud. Bella, may the Lord bless her, just smiled and lay on the couch. Sonya, who had been expressionless, flew into a rage, ready to kill Randell. Yesterday, I thought she would! [2184] I've done my best. I talked to Sonya and to Randell, trying to get them to think rationally. When they got together, they started arguing again: you can't imagine how boring it was. Randell has been trying to find out more about Stanforth, and it seems that he's no good at all. [2185] Meanwhile, the business has been neglected. I've been working in the office, and in a way, it's been quite interesting because Randell has let me do it. Yesterday, he said to me, \"Thank God, there's at least one sane person in the world. You wouldn't fall in love with a scoundrel, would you, Blatch?\" I said I didn't think I would. Randell said, \"Let's discuss some of the minor issues in London.\" He can be a mischievous devil at times, and he can be hasty in a crisis. [2186] \"Are you determined to be an honest person, Blatch?\" he said one day. And I was indeed intending to be so! I cannot understand how people can ignore deceit, but Randall is really not clear-sighted. He only knows what is truly illegal. [2187] Bella just laughs at all this. She thinks that the whole Sonya affair is nonsense. \"Sonya has money of her own,\" she said, \"Why shouldn't she marry the man if she wants to?\" I said that this marriage would be a terrible mistake, and Bella said, \"Marriage to a man you love can never be a mistake \u2013 even if you regret it later.\" She also said, \"I think Sonya doesn't want to quarrel with Randall for the money. She really likes money.\" [2188] That's the situation now. How is your father? I will not say \"with regards\" to him. But if you think it's good, you can say it. Have you met more people lately? Dear, you cannot always be so sickly. Sonya sent her regards. She just came in, clenching and unclenching her hands, like an angry cat sharpening its claws. I think she had another argument with Randall. Of course, Sonya is good at stirring up trouble. She always stares at you with a calm gaze that makes you dare not look back at her. [2189] Your sister deeply loves you, dear, and you must pull yourself together. This iodine therapy will be different. I have been consulting with others, and it seems that iodine therapy is indeed very effective. [2190] Yours sincerely, Letitia\n[2191] Miss Maple folded the letter and handed it back to the inspector. Her expression was somewhat absent-minded. [2192] \"What do you think of her?\" Colonel Corder urged. \"What impression do you get of her?\" [2193] \"About Sonya? It's difficult to see one person through the eyes of another... She has decided to take her share - I think that's for sure. And she wants to be on top in both worlds...\"\n[2194] \" 'As if an angry cat, clenching and unclenching its hands,' Colonel Corder muttered. \"You know, that phrase reminds me of someone...\"\n[2195] He frowned. [2196] \"Consultations...\" Miss Marple murmured. [2197] \"I wish we could get the results of those consultations,\" Cora said. [2198] \"Does this letter remind you of anything at St. Mary Mead?\" Round asked, but her words were muffled by the safety pin in her mouth. [2199] \"I'm not sure, dear... Dr. Blacklock might be a bit like the missionary Mr. Curtis in Wychwood-on-Lea. He wouldn't let his children wear braces. He said if their teeth grew crooked, that was God's will. 'After all,' I told him, 'you shave and get your hair cut. Letting your hair grow long might be God's will too.' He said that was different. Classic machismo. But it doesn't help us with our current problem.\" [2200] \"We haven't been able to trace the revolver. It wasn't Rudi Schlesinger's. If we could find out who in Chipping Cleghorn had a revolver...\"\n[2201] \"Captain Eastbrook has one,\" Round said. \"It's in the drawer where he keeps his collars.\" [2202] \"How do you know that, Mrs. Harmon?\" [2203] \"Mrs. Bartlett told me, she's my weekly maid. Or more precisely, she comes twice a week. She said, as a retired gentleman, he would naturally have a revolver, and if a burglar came in, he could grab it on the spur of the moment.\" [2204] \"When did she tell you that?\" [2205] \"A long time ago. I think it was about six months ago.\" [2206] \"Captain Eastbrook?\" murmured Colonel Corcoran. [2207] \"It's like playing a game of chance,\" said Round, with a pin in her mouth. \"Spin and spin, and each time something different comes up.\" [2208] \"That's true,\" groaned Colonel Corcoran. [2209] \"One day Captain Eastbrook came to the lodge to drop off some books. He might have been putting oil on the door then. Even though he was upfront about going there, unlike Miss Chinchcliff.\" [2210] Miss Maple coughed lightly. \"You'll have to forgive our time, Inspector.\" [2211] Colonel Cordeaux looked at her in confusion. [2212] \"After all,\" Miss Maple said, \"you're a policeman, aren't you? People can't possibly tell the police everything, can they?\" [2213] \"I don't see why not,\" Colonel Cordeaux said, \"unless they want to hide a crime.\" [2214] \"She means butter,\" Roundy said, struggling to crawl around a table leg and pin down a floating piece of paper, \"exchanging butter and corn for a hen, sometimes cream \u2013 even sometimes bacon.\" [2215] \"Show him Miss Blacklock's note,\" Miss Maple said, \"It's been a while, but it reads like a first-rate mystery story.\" [2216] \"Where did I put it? You mean this one, Aunt Jane?\" [2217] Miss Marple took the note and looked at it. [2218] \"Yes,\" she said contentedly. \"That's the one.\" [2219] She handed the note to the Inspector. [2220] Blacklock wrote:\n[2221] I made some inquiries - any time after three o'clock on Thursday. [2222] If there's any for me, leave it in the usual place. [2223] Roundy spat out the pin and laughed. Miss Marple watched the inspector's face. [2224] The vicar's wife rushed to explain: \"Thursday is butter-making day at a farm nearby. They let friends have a bit. Usually Miss Chinchcliffe goes to get it. She's on good terms with the farmers, I suppose because she keeps pigs. But it's all done in secret, you know, like a kind of local barter. One gets cream and then sends something else back - cucumbers or something like that - or adds something else when the pig is killed. Occasionally, an animal meets with an accident and has to be destroyed. Oh, you know the sort of thing. People can't tell the police, of course. I suppose many of these barter transactions are illegal - but nobody is quite sure, because the law is so complicated. But I imagine Miss Chinchcliffe took a pound of cream and slipped it into the usual place. By the way, the usual place is the box under the sideboard where the flour is kept. But there was no flour in it.\" [2225] Cora Dedalus sighed. [2226] \"I'm glad I came to you ladies,\" he said. [2227] \"There used to be cloth coupons,\" said Roundabout, \"which couldn't be sold because that would be seen as dishonest. They couldn't be exchanged for money. But people like Mrs. Bartlett, Mrs. Finch, and Mrs. Higgins, if they liked a nice woolen sweater or a winter coat that wasn't too worn out, would pay for it with a cloth coupon instead of money.\" [2228] \"You had better not say any more,\" said Corridor. \"It's all illegal.\" [2229] \"There shouldn't be such silly laws,\" said Roundabout, and popped the pin back in her mouth. \"Of course, I didn't do it, because Julian didn't like me doing that sort of thing, so I didn't. But I knew all about it, of course.\" [2230] A sense of despair overcame the inspector. [2231] \"It all sounds so pleasant and ordinary,\" he said, \"so amusing and simple.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1702] \"And I've always thought,\" she said, \"that being a man must be terribly boring.\" [1703] Then she said thoughtfully, \"I think Randall mainly saw Blanche as a brother. He relied on her judgment, and her judgment was always so good. You know, she's helped him out of difficulties more than once.\" [1704] \"She told me she saved him once with her money?\" [1705] \"Yes, but I mean more than that. After all these years, I can tell the truth. Randall can't tell right from wrong, he's slow to feel, this poor dear doesn't know what cunning or deceit is. Blanche kept him from going astray. Letitia Blacklock has a quality that is absolutely upright. She would never do anything dishonest. Her character is excellent, you know. I've always admired her. They had a hard life when they were girls. Their father was a country doctor - slow-witted and obstinate - a tyrant in the house. Letitia ran away to England and trained to be a certified accountant. Her sister is somewhat disabled, probably deformed somewhere, so she never sees anyone and stays at home. So, when the old man died, Letitia gave up everything and went home to take care of her sister. Randall was angry with her - but it didn't matter. Once Letitia decides what is her responsibility, she will not be swayed, no matter how you argue with her.\" [1706] \"How long before your husband died?\" [1707] \"Two or three years, I think. Randall left his will before he died, and it hasn't been changed. He said to me, 'We have no children.' (Our little boy, you know, died when he was two.) 'After we're gone, it would be best for Blanche to take the money. She'll make a name for herself, you'll see.'\" [1708] \"You see,\" Bella continued, \"Randall enjoyed making money - not just having it - but the excitement, the risks, and the thrill. Blanche liked all that too. She has the same adventurous spirit and the same decisiveness. Poor thing, she's never experienced those ordinary pleasures - falling in love, leading a man around by the nose, testing them - building a family, raising children, enjoying the real pleasures of life.\" [1709] Kradok was surprised: a woman who had suffered from chronic illness her whole life, her only child had died, and her husband had died. She lived a lonely widowhood and had been a hopeless invalid for years. Yet she had genuine compassion for others and scorned pain. [1710] She nodded at him. [1711] \"I know what you're thinking. But I have everything that makes life worth living - I may have lost it all - but I once had it. I was pretty and happy when I was a girl, I married the man I loved, and he never stopped loving me... As for children, he died, but we spent two precious years together... I've suffered a lot physically - but because of the pain, you learn to appreciate the wonderful joy that comes when the pain stops. Moreover, everyone has always been good to me... I am a lucky woman, really.\" [1712] Cora Dedalus found an opening in what she had just said. [1713] \"Ms. Goddard, you said that your husband left the money to Miss Blacklock because he had no other heirs. But that's not entirely true, is it? He had a sister.\" [1714] \"Ah, Sonya. They had a falling out years ago and have been estranged ever since.\" [1715] \"He didn't agree with her marriage?\" [1716] \"Yes, she married a man - what was his name -\"\n[1717] \"Stanfordtis.\" [1718] \"That's him, Dimetri Stanfordtis. Randall always thought he was a fraud. The two men never liked each other from the start. But Sonya was madly in love with him and wanted to marry him. I couldn't understand why she couldn't just marry him. Men's views on such things are always so strange. Sonya was no longer a little girl - she was twenty-five, and she knew what she was doing. He was a fraud, I'm sure - I mean, he was a genuine fraud. I believe he had a criminal record - Randall always suspected he wasn't using his real name. All of this Sonya knew. The problem was - Randall couldn't agree - that Dimetri was a man who was extremely attractive to women, and he loved Sonya just as much as she loved him. Randall insisted he married her for the money - but that wasn't the case. Sonya was very pretty, you know, and ambitious. If the marriage didn't work out, if Dimetri didn't treat her well or was unfaithful, she would have left to minimize the loss. She was a wealthy woman who could live as she pleased.\" [1719] \"Did their rift ever resolve?\" [1720] \"No. Randall and Sonya never got along well. She hated him for trying to stop the marriage. She said, 'Well, you're so unreasonable! I'll never speak to you again!'\" [1721] \"But it wasn't like that for you, was it?\" [1722] Bella smiled. \"Eighteen months later, I received a letter from her. I remember it was from Budapest, but she didn't leave an address. She told me to tell Randall that she was extremely happy and had a pair of twins.\" [1723] \"Did she tell you their names?\" [1724] Bella smiled again. \"She said they were born just after noon \u2014 she planned to name them Pip and Emma [2]. Of course, these names could have been a joke.\" [1725] \"Did you hear from her again after that?\" [1726] \"No. She said she and her husband were going to take their little ones to America for a while. And then I never heard from her again\u2026\"\n[1727] \"Do you happen to still have that letter?\" [1728] \"No, I'm afraid I don't. I told Randall about the letter, and he just grumbled, 'There's always going to be a day when she regrets marrying that guy.' That's all he said about it. We had actually forgotten about her. She was out of our lives\u2026\"\n[1729] \"But Mr. Goddard left his property to her children in case Miss Blacklock passed away before you?\" [1730] \"Oh, that was my idea. When he told me about the will, I said, 'What if I die before her?' He was surprised. I said, 'Well, I know Blacklock is as strong as a horse, and I'm a delicate flower - but you know, accidents can happen, and squeaky doors last longer.' Then he said, 'There's no one else - none at all.' So I said, 'Well, there's Sonya.' He immediately retorted, 'Let that guy have my money? No way!' I said, 'Well, then give it to her children. Pip and Emma, and there might be more by now.' So he grumbled and grumbled, but he added that in.\" [1731] \"Since then,\" Corrodick said slowly, \"you haven't heard anything about your sister-in-law and her children?\" [1732] \"No \u2013 they may be dead, or \u2013 anywhere.\" [1733] They might be in Chipping Cheddar, Corrodick thought. [1734] Bella Goddard seemed to read his mind, and her eyes widened in surprise. She said, \"Don't let them hurt Blanche. Blanche is a good person \u2013 very good \u2013 you must stop them from hurting her \u2013\"\n[1735] Her voice suddenly cut off. Corrodick saw a gray shadow appear in the corners of her mouth and eyes. [1736] \"You are tired,\" he said, \"I must leave.\" [1737] She nodded. [1738] \"Call Mac[3] in,\" she whispered, \"Yes...I am tired...\"\n[1739] Her hand weakly moved. \"Take care of Blairch...never let anything happen to her...take care of her...\"\n[1740] \"I will do my best, Mrs. Goodlady.\" He stood up and walked towards the door. [1741] Her voice was so faint it was like a thread, coming from behind him...\n[1742] \"Time is short... Before I die... She is in danger... Take care of her...\"\n[1743] As he went out, the MacLane nurse passed him and he spoke uneasily, \"I hope I haven't caused her any harm.\" [1744] \"Oh, I don't think so, Mr. Corrado,\" she said, \"I told you she would suddenly tire.\" [1745] Later, he asked the nurse, \"There's just one thing I didn't get to ask Mrs. Goddard... Do you happen to have any photos of her from the past? If so, I'd like to...\"\n[1746] She interrupted him. [1747] \"I'm afraid there's nothing like that. All her personal documents and belongings were kept in the London mansion during the early days of the war. Mrs. Goddard was very ill at the time. Everything saved there was destroyed during the Blitz. Mrs. Goddard was very upset about losing so many personal mementos and family documents. I'm afraid there's nothing left here.\" [1748] So that's that, thought Corrado. [1749] However, he felt the trip was not in vain. The ghosts of Pip and Emma, the twin siblings, were not mere figments. [1750] Corrado thought. \"There are a pair of siblings raised in Europe. Mrs. Goddard was a wealthy woman when she married, but money dispersed quickly in Europe at that time. During the war, economic fluctuations were very unusual. These two young people, the children of the man with the criminal record, were left almost penniless in England. What would they do? Look for the whereabouts of all their wealthy relatives. Their uncle, a wealthy millionaire, had passed away. So the first thing they would do is look for the will to see if they or their mother happened to inherit the money. They went to the law firm and learned the contents of the will. Then they may have also learned about Miss Letitia Blacklock, who is still alive. They inquired about the situation of Mrs. Randall Goddard, the widow. She was a sick woman living in Scotland and they learned she was not long for this world. If Miss Letitia Blacklock died before her, they would inherit a large fortune. So what would they do next?\" [1751] Cora Dedek continued to ponder: \"They won't go to Scotland. They'll find out where Miss Letitia Blacklock is living now and then go there \u2013 but not under their real identities\u2026 They'll go together \u2013 or separately? Emma\u2026 I really want to know\u2026 Pip and Emma, if either of them isn't in Chipping Cleghorn, I'll eat my hat\u2026\"\n[1752] * * *\n[1753] [1] The nickname of Blacklock. [1754] [2] Pip and Emma, when put together, mean \"afternoon\" in English. [1755] [3] The abbreviation of McLean. [1756] Chapter 15: The Death of a Delicacy\n[1757]1\n[1758] In the kitchen of the small estate, Miss Blacklock was giving instructions to Midge. [1759] \"Tomato sandwiches and sardine sandwiches, and your excellent scones, and I want you to make your specialty cake.\" [1760] \"You want so much, is it a gathering?\" [1761] \"It's Miss Bonner's birthday, some people are coming for tea.\" [1762] \"At her age, people don't celebrate birthdays, it's best to forget.\" [1763] \"But she doesn't want to forget! Some people are going to give her presents \u2013 so we're having a little party, it will be nice.\" [1764] \"You said the same last time \u2013 and look what happened!\" [1765] Miss Blacklock managed to restrain herself from reacting. [1766] \"Well, there won't be anything this time.\" [1767] \"How do you know what will happen in this house? I'm always trembling, and at night I lock the door and look into the wardrobe to see if anyone is hiding in there.\" [1768] \"That will certainly make you feel better and safer,\" Miss Blacklock said coldly. [1769] \"The cake you want me to make \u2014 is it the kind \u2014\" Midge uttered a sound that, to Miss Blacklock's ears accustomed to English, sounded like \"sweating\" in German, or like cats spitting at each other. [1770] \"Yes, that kind. It's quite heavy.\" [1771] \"Well, it's rather greasy. But I have nothing to make such a cake. I need chocolate, lots of cream, sugar, and raisins.\" [1772] \"You can use the can of cream that was sent from America, and the raisins we were going to save for Christmas, there's a thick piece of chocolate and a pound of sugar here.\" [1773] Midge's face lit up with a radiant smile. [1774] \"So, on your behalf, I'll do it,\" she exclaimed happily, \"it will be sweet and creamy, melt-in-your-mouth! I'll put chocolate frosting on top - I'll do a good job, and I'll write good wishes on it. These British cakes taste like sand, they've never even tasted such a cake. Delicious, they'll say - oh, so delicious!\" [1775] Her face clouded over again. [1776] \"Mr. Patrick called it the 'death of deliciousness.' My cake! I don't want anyone to call it that!\" [1777] \"He was actually complimenting you,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"He meant that it's so good, it's worth dying for.\" [1778] Micky looked at her skeptically. [1779] \"But I don't like the word 'die.' They won't die because of my cake, no, they'll feel very, very good\u2026\"\n[1780] \"I'm sure we will.\" [1781] Miss Blacklock turned away from the kitchen, relieved that the negotiations had gone well. Mickey was impossible to predict. [1782] She met Dora Bunner in the hallway outside the kitchen. [1783] \"Oh, Letty, shall I go in and tell Mickey how to cut the sandwiches?\" [1784] \"Don't go,\" said Miss Blacklock, firmly leading her friend into the hallway, \"she's in a good mood now, and I don't want to disturb her.\" [1785] \"But I can teach her how to\u2014\"\n[1786] \"Please don't teach her anything, Dora. These Central Europeans don't like being told what to do, they hate it.\" [1787] Dora looked at her inquiringly and then suddenly smiled. [1788] \"Edmund Swithinham just phoned. He wished me a happy birthday and said he'd bring a jar of honey as a present this afternoon. How kind, isn't it? I can't imagine how he would know it's my birthday.\" [1789] \"It seems everyone knows. You must have been talking about it, Dora.\" [1790] \"Oh, I just happened to mention that I turned fifty-nine today\u2014\"\n[1791] \"You're sixty-four,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled with amusement. [1792] \"But Miss Quincannon said, 'You don't look your age. How old do you guess I am?' That was a very awkward question, because Miss Quincannon looks so odd, she could be any age. She said she'd bring some eggs along for me. I told her our hens haven't been laying many eggs lately.\" [1793] \"You've done quite well for a birthday, Dora,\" Miss Blacklock said, \"honey, eggs\u2014and Julia brought a whole box of chocolates\u2014\"\n[1794] \"I have no idea where she got that stuff.\" [1795] \"Best not to ask. Her methods are, strictly speaking, illegal.\" [1796] \"And your lovely brooch,\" said Miss Bunn, lowering her head and looking proudly at the small diamond leaf pinned to her chest. [1797] \"Do you like it? I'm glad. I never liked jewellery.\" [1798] \"I do like it.\" [1799] \"Good. Let's go and feed the ducks.\" [1800] 2\n[1801] \"Ha!\" Patrick called out solemnly as the birthday dinner began around the dining table, \"What is this before me? Delicious death.\" [1802] \"Shh,\" Miss Blacklock hushed, \"Don't let Midge hear you call her cake that. She'll be offended.\" [1803] \"But it is delicious death! This is Bonnie's birthday cake?\" [1804] \"Yes,\" Miss Bunn said, \"I'm enjoying the most delightful birthday.\" [1805] Her cheeks flushed with excitement. After Captain Eastbrook presented her with a box of candy, bowed, and said, \"Sweet sugar for a sweetheart!\" she had been like this ever since. [1806] When the colonel paid her a compliment, Julia turned her face away sharply, causing Miss Blacklock to frown. [1807] After the main course was finished, they came around with a round of cookies, and then everyone stood up from their seats. [1808] \"I feel a little unwell,\" Julia said, \"It must be because of the cake. I remember feeling the same last time.\" [1809] \"It's worth it,\" Patrick said. [1810] \"These foreigners certainly know their way around pastries,\" Miss Chinchcliff said, \"They just don't know how to make a proper boiled pudding.\" [1811] Everyone respected and didn't express their opinions, although Patrick had a sentence on his lips, wondering if anyone really wanted to eat a plain steamed pudding. [1812] \"Did you hire a new gardener?\" Miss Chickchaff asked Miss Blacklock when they returned to the living room. [1813] \"No, what's the matter?\" [1814] \"I saw a man peering around the chicken coop. He looked very proud and like a soldier.\" [1815] \"Oh, that's our detective,\" Julia said. [1816] Mrs. Eastbrook dropped her handbag upon hearing this. [1817] \"Detective?\" she exclaimed. \"But\u2014but\u2014why?\" [1818] \"I don't know,\" Julia said. \"He was walking around, staring at the house. I suppose he was protecting Aunt Letty.\" [1819] \"Nonsense,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"I can take care of myself, thank you.\" [1820] \"Although the matter is probably over,\" Mrs. Eastbrook called out. \"But I still want to ask, why did they stop questioning?\" [1821] \"The police weren't satisfied,\" her husband replied, \"That's all.\" [1822] \"But what weren't they satisfied with?\" [1823] Colonel Eastbrook shook his head, with an air of knowing more than he was letting on.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1259] \"Let me think... 1922... No\u2014I can't remember... I guess they're around twenty-six or so.\" [1260] Her face twitched. \"But you don't think\u2014\"\n[1261] \"I think someone shot at you with intent to kill you. I think the same person or people will try again. I hope, if you're willing, you'll be extremely, extremely careful, Miss Blacklock. They've planned one murder, but it didn't work. I think another murder will happen soon.\" [1262] 2\n[1263] Philippa Hammersley straightened up, pushing a strand of wet hair from her forehead. She was weeding a flower bed. [1264] \"Inspector?\" [1265] She looked at him curiously, while he looked at her more closely than before. She was pretty, with pale gold hair, a long face, and a very English look about her, with a determined chin and lips. There was a suppressed and tense quality about her. Her eyes were a deep blue, steady, and uncommunicative. Just the kind\u2014thought Corrado\u2014of girl who would keep a secret. [1266] \"I'm always bothering you when you're working, Mrs. Havers. I feel very sorry about that. But I didn't want to wait until you went in to lunch. Besides, out here, away from the house, I thought it would be more comfortable for us to talk.\" [1267] \"Is it, Inspector?\" [1268] There was no emotion or interest in her voice. But there seemed to be a note of caution - or was it just his imagination? [1269] \"Something was told to me this morning. It concerns you.\" [1270] Phyllida raised her eyebrows slightly. [1271] \"You tell me, Mrs. Himmelstoss, that you don't know Rudolf Schrader?\" [1272] \"Not bad.\" [1273] \"You also said that you saw him die there, which was the first time you saw him. Is that right?\" [1274] \"Of course. I've never seen him before.\" [1275] \"Have you, for example, ever spoken to him in the pavilion at the small encampment?\" [1276] \"In the gazebo?\" [1277] He was almost certain he had detected a hint of fear in her voice. [1278] \"Yes, Mrs. Hemes.\" [1279] \"Who said that?\" [1280] \"I found out that you talked to this person, Rudi Scherz. He asked where you could hide, and you said you would show him, and mentioned six fifteen. The time when the robbery took place was six fifteen, and that's when Scherz arrived here from the bus stop.\" [1281] There was a silence. Then Phila let out a short burst of laughter, looking amused. [1282] \"I have no idea who told you that,\" she said, \"but I can guess. This person is so stupid, clumsy, and vicious - of course, for some reason, Micky hates me more than she hates anyone else.\" [1283] \"You deny this accusation?\" [1284] \"Of course it's not true... I've never seen Rudi Scherz in my life, and I didn't go near the gazebo that morning. I was working here.\" [1285] The inspector asked in a friendly tone:\n[1286] \"Which morning?\" [1287] There was another pause. She blinked her eyelashes. [1288] \"Every morning. I'm here every morning. I leave at one o'clock.\" [1289] She added mockingly, \"You shouldn't believe Midge. She always lies.\" [1290] \"So that's it,\" Corradino said as he left with Fletcher. \"Two young women telling different stories. Which one should I believe?\" [1291] \"Everyone seems to agree that the foreign girl is always lying,\" Fletcher said. \"From my experience dealing with foreigners, lying comes more easily than telling the truth. It's clear she has a grudge against Mrs. Hammers.\" [1292] \"So you'd believe Mrs. Hammers, then?\" [1293] \"Unless you have a reason not to, sir.\" [1294] Cordeaux had none, not really\u2014only the overly calm blue eyes and the fluid way she spoke of that morning. For his part, he couldn't recall whether the conversation in the gazebo had taken place in the morning or the afternoon. [1295] After all, Miss Blacklock\u2014or Miss Bonner\u2014might have mentioned a young foreigner who had come to ask for money to return to Switzerland. Therefore, Mrs. Philips might have assumed the conversation had taken place that morning. [1296] However, Kollautz still felt that there was a tone of fear in her voice when she asked, \"In the pavilion?\" [1297] He decided not to draw any conclusions from this. [1298]3\n[1299] The garden of the clergyman is particularly pleasant. A sudden warm flow of autumn descended upon England. Inspector Corrado could not recall the exact day when the Indian summer appeared, whether it was on St. Martin's Day or St. Luke's Day, but he felt that day was very pleasant and made him feel soft all over. [1300] He sat in the recliner, which was given to him by the energetic and round woman named Round, who was going to attend a gathering of mothers. Miss Marple wrapped herself in a shawl and draped a large blanket over her knees, sitting beside him and knitting. The warm sunlight, the tranquil garden, and the rhythmic tapping of Miss Marple's knitting needles lulled the inspector into a drowsy state. However, at the same time, a nightmare-like feeling stirred within him. [1301] It seemed like a familiar dream, so peaceful and comfortable, but a dangerous undercurrent was constantly growing, and comfort eventually turned into terror...\n[1302] \"You shouldn't have come here,\" he blurted out. [1303] Miss Marple's knitting stopped for a moment. Her jade-like eyes looked at him calmly and thoughtfully. [1304] She said, \"I understand what you mean. You are a responsible young man. But everything here is fine. Round Round's father is our parish vicar, an excellent scholar; his mother is a truly remarkable woman - she has real spiritual strength. They are both my old friends. So, whenever I come to Meddenham, I will come here and stay with Round Round for a while, and that is the most natural thing in the world. [1305] \"Oh, maybe,\" said Coradine, \"but - but don't snoop around... I have a feeling, really, that it's not safe to do so.\" [1306] Miss Marple smiled slightly. [1307] \"But I'm afraid,\" she said, \"that we old ladies always like to pry. If I didn't, it would be strange, and that would attract attention. Asking about the friends of my acquaintance in various places, chatting about whether they remember someone or whether they remember the name of the woman whose daughter is married. Such questions are always helpful, aren't they, in finding out whether people are really as they say they are?\" [1308] \"Helpful?\" asked the inspector, bewildered. [1309] \"Helpful in finding out whether people are really as they say they are,\" said Miss Marple. [1310] She continued, \"Because it's this very thing that worries you, isn't it? Since the war began, the world has changed in a particular way. For example, Chipping Cleghorn is very much like the place where I live, St. Mary Mead. Fifteen years ago, everyone knew everyone else's business. The families of the Bannetts, the Hartneills, the Price-Reedleys, the Weatherbys\u2026 their parents, grandparents, aunts, and uncles had lived there for generations. If a stranger came to live there, they usually had an introduction, or they had served together in the same regiment or on the same ship. If they were a complete stranger, goodness, everyone would want to know all about them before they felt satisfied.\" [1311] She paused and nodded slowly. [1312] \"But it's not like that now. Every village is full of people who have no local connections and just settle down. The big houses are sold, the little cottages are bought and sold, and people just turn up \u2013 with no proof of who they are except what they say. You see, they come from all over the world \u2013 India, Hong Kong, China; people who were born in France, living in cheap houses in Italy and on strange islands; people who have enough money to retire. But they don't know each other. You can have a house full of things from Benares, and talk about \"Tiffany\" and \"Jota Hakizuri\" [1] \u2013 and have paintings from Taormina on the wall, but talk about British churches and libraries \u2013 just like Miss Inchcliff and Miss Muggleton. You might come from France, or have spent half your life in the East. Everyone accepts newcomers without a second thought. No one expects a letter of introduction from a friend saying that someone is a good person, someone from childhood\u2026 and so on.\" [1313] And this, thought Cuddlecome, was the source of his worry. He couldn't understand. People were just faces and personalities, identified by ration books and identity cards\u2026 but with no photos or fingerprints. Anyone could get a suitable identity card \u2013 the ties that once bound the English countryside together were gone, and this was part of the reason. In towns, no one knows their neighbors. In the countryside, it's the same, but sometimes you feel like you do. And because of that faulty door, Cuddlecome knew that there was a neighbor in Lady Blakelock's drawing room who was far from the friendly exterior she showed\u2026\n[1314] So he was worried about Miss Marple. She was so wise, yet so frail and old\u2026\n[1315]\n[1316] \"In a way, we can check these people \u2026\" he said, but he knew it wouldn't be easy \u2026 India, China, Hong Kong, southern France \u2026 it's much harder than fifteen years ago. He knew that some people were using borrowed identity cards to travel around \u2013 mostly from those who had died unexpectedly in the city. There are organized groups that buy identity cards and forged ration cards and identity cards to defraud, and there have been over a hundred cases. You can check, but it takes time, and he lacked time because Mrs. Randall-Godley's days were numbered. [1317] So, although Kroll was anxious and tired, drowsy from the sun, he told Miss Marple about Randall-Godley and Pick and Emma. [1318] \"Just two names,\" he said, \"just nicknames! The people who use these names may not exist, or they may be respectable citizens living somewhere in Europe. On the other hand, the people with these names may be one or both of them in Chipping Cleghorn.\" [1319] About twenty-five years old \u2013 who fits this description? He continued, \"Her niece and nephew \u2013 or cousins \u2013 I want to know how long she hasn't seen those two \u2026\"\n[1320] \"I'll try to find out,\" Miss Marple said quietly. [1321] \"For God's sake, Miss Marple, don't...\"\n[1322] \"It's not difficult at all, Inspector, you really don't need to worry. And it won't be conspicuous, because, you see, it won't be official. If there's anything to worry about, you wouldn't want them to be on their guard, would you?\" [1323] Pip and Emma, thought Colonel Corcoran. Pip and Emma? He was haunted by Pip and Emma. The charming and audacious young man and the beautiful yet cool-eyed girl...\n[1324] \"In the next forty-eight hours, I may have more information on them,\" he said. \"Mrs. Goddard might be able to provide information about them if she can speak.\" [1325] \"I think that's wise,\" Miss Marple said hesitantly. \"I hope you've warned Blacklock Miss to be careful?\" [1326] \"Yes, I've warned her. And I'm leaving someone to keep an eye on things here.\" [1327] Miss Maple's look made it clear that it would be useless to rely on the police if danger was in the house, but Cora Crofton avoided her gaze. [1328] \"Remember,\" Cora Crofton said, looking straight at her, \"I've warned you too.\" [1329] \"I assure you, Inspector,\" Miss Maple said, \"I'll take care of myself.\" [1330] * * *\n[1331] [1] Tiffin and jottah are both Indian English terms, the former meaning lunch and the latter meaning a light breakfast. [1332] Chapter 11: Tea Time Chatter\n[1333] If Letitia Blacklock seemed a little absent-minded when Mrs. Harmon brought her guests over for tea, it was almost impossible for the other guest - Miss Marple - to notice, as they were meeting for the first time. [1334] This elderly lady's gossipy manner was charming. She almost immediately showed herself to be the kind of elderly woman who is constantly on the lookout for thieves. [1335] \"My dear,\" she assured the hostess, \"they could come in anywhere. They're everywhere now, even with all the new American methods. I still believe in the old-fashioned devices: door hooks and a pair of bright eyes. They can pick locks and slip open door latches, but a copper hook and a pair of eyes can thwart them. Have you tried it?\" [1336] \"We're not very good with door latches and copper hooks,\" Miss Blacklock replied cheerfully, \"and there's not much to steal in the house anyway.\" [1337] \"Chain the front door,\" Miss Marple suggested, \"and have the maid open it just a crack to see who's there before they can rush in.\" [1338] \"I think Mimi, our Central European, would like that idea.\" [1339] \"The robbery must have been absolutely terrifying for you,\" Miss Marple said, \"and roundy has been telling me all about it.\" [1340] \"I was paralyzed with fear,\" roundy said. [1341] \"It was a dreadful experience,\" Miss Blacklock admitted. [1342] \"He tripped and shot himself, it seems, which was probably divine intervention. Thieves are so ruthless nowadays. How did he get in?\" [1343] \"Oh, I'm afraid we don't lock the door very often.\" [1344] \"Oh, Letty,\" Miss Bonner exclaimed, \"I forgot to tell you, the Inspector was very strange this morning. He insisted on opening the second door\u2014you know, the one that wouldn't open\u2014and he was looking for a key to unlock it, and he said it had been oiled, but I don't understand why, because\u2014\"\n[1345] By the time she saw Miss Blacklock's gesture to stop talking, it was too late, so she stopped short, but her mouth was still open. [1346] \"Oh, Letty, I'm so\u2014I mean, oh, I'm terribly sorry, Letty\u2014oh, dear, I'm such a fool.\" [1347] \"It's all right,\" Miss Blacklock said, but she was annoyed. \"I just think Chief Inspector Corridick doesn't want anyone to talk about it. I didn't know you were there, Mr. Harmon, when he was doing his experiment. Can you understand that, Mrs. Harmon?\" [1348] \"Ah, yes,\" said Round, \"we won't tell a soul, will we, Aunt Jane? But I'm wondering why\u2014\"\n[1349] She fell into a reverie. Miss Bonner sat fidgeting, looking miserable, and finally couldn't resist blurting out, \"I always say the wrong thing, oh, dear, Letty, I only make things worse for you.\" [1350] Miss Blacklock quickly said, \"You're my greatest comfort, Mr. Harmon. And, after all, there aren't many secrets in a small place like Chipping Cleghorn.\" [1351] \"Indeed, there isn't,\" said Miss Marple. \"You know, the way news gets about here is most extraordinary. The servants, of course, are one thing, but it's not only that, because there aren't so many servants nowadays. And then there are the women who come in to do the housework, and they're probably worse, because they go about and spread it all over the place.\" [1352] \"Ah!\" said roundly. \"I see now! Of course, if that door could be opened, someone could slip out in the dark and rob - only it couldn't be - because it was the man from the Royal Baths who did it. Or was it?... No, I still don't understand...\"\n[1353] \"So it all happened in this room?\" asked Miss Marple, and then added apologetically, \"I'm afraid you'll think me incurably curious, Miss Blacklock - but it's so exciting - like reading a story in the paper - I just want to hear it all through, from beginning to end, if you understand what I mean -\"\n[1354] At once, she heard roundly and Miss Bunner talking in a confused and rambling way, with Miss Blacklock occasionally correcting them. [1355] In the meantime, Patrick came in, kindly participating in the recapitulation - and even playing the part of Rudi Schlesinger. [1356] \"Aunt Letty is there\u2014in the corner of the arch\u2014stand there, Aunt Letty.\" [1357] Miss Blacklock obeyed, and they pointed out the bullet holes to Miss Marple. [1358] \"How remarkable\u2014what a narrow escape!\" she breathed. [1359] \"I was just going to give the guests their cigarettes,\" said Miss Blacklock, indicating the large silver cigarette box on the table. [1360] \"People are so careless with cigarettes,\" said Miss Bonner disapprovingly. \"No one appreciates good furniture nowadays. Someone put a cigarette on this lovely table, look at it, how badly it's been burned. It's disgraceful.\" [1361] Miss Blacklock sighed. [1362] \"Sometimes, I think you care too much about other people's things.\" [1363] Miss Bunner loved her friend's possessions so fiercely that it seemed as if she were the true owner herself. Round was always fond of this very charming trait in Dora, and showed no signs of jealousy. [1364] \"It's a lovely table,\" Miss Marple said politely. \"And that ceramic lamp is so pretty.\" [1365] Miss Bunner, the recipient of the compliment, seemed to own the lamp herself, rather than Miss Blacklock. [1366] \"Very pretty, isn't it? Dresden. There's another pair, I think, in the other room.\" [1367] \"You know where everything is in the house, Dora - or you think you do,\" said Miss Blacklock in a friendly tone. \"You care for my things more than I do.\" [1368] Miss Bonner blushed. [1369] \"I do like nice things,\" she said, half defensively, half wistfully.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nMiss Marple turned her head and saw Miss Blacklock standing behind them\u2014she must have just come in. [1597] \"Coffee and gossip, Dora?\" said Miss Blacklock, with a reproachful tone. \"Good morning, Miss Marple. It's so cold, isn't it?\" [1598] \"We were just talking,\" said Miss Bunner hastily, \"there are so many rules and regulations now that it's hard to keep track of them all.\" [1599] The door burst open, and Round swung into \"The Bluebird.\" [1600] \"Hello,\" she called, \"did I miss the coffee?\" [1601] \"No, dear,\" said Miss Marple. \"Sit down and have some.\" [1602] \"We must be going home,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"Have you finished shopping, Bonnie?\" [1603] Her voice was once again filled with condescension, but her eyes still held a hint of reproach. [1604] \"Yes, yes, thank you, Letty,\" said Miss Bunce. \"I need to stop by the pharmacy for some aspirin and a blister plaster.\" [1605] After the door to \"The Bluebird\" closed behind them, Round asked, \"What were you talking about?\" [1606] Miss Marple didn't answer right away. After she had finished her tea, she said, \"Family ties are a very powerful thing. Very powerful. Do you remember that famous case? I can't remember which one it was. They said the husband poisoned the wife, and put the poison in a glass of wine. At the trial, the daughter said she drank half of her mother's glass - which negated the charge against the father. They did say - though perhaps it was just a rumor - that after that, she never spoke to her father again and never lived with him again. Of course, a father is one thing, but a nephew or cousin is another. But it's still the same - no one wants their family to be hanged, right?\" [1607] \"Yes,\" Round thought for a moment. \"I don't think they would.\" [1608] Miss Marple leaned back in her chair and murmured to herself, \"People are so alike, no matter where they are.\" [1609] \"Who do I look like?\" [1610] \"Well, dear, to be honest, you look like yourself. I can't think of anyone else you remind me of, except perhaps -\"\n[1611] \"You're doing it again,\" said Round Round. [1612] \"I was just thinking of one of my former parlour maids, dear.\" [1613] \"I'd be a terrible maid,\" said Round Round. [1614] \"Yes, dear, she was too. She wasn't very good at serving others at the table. The table was always a mess, the kitchen knives mixed with the dining room knives, and her hat - this was a long time ago - she never wore it properly.\" [1615] Round Round unconsciously adjusted her hat. [1616] \"What happened next?\" she asked eagerly. [1617] \"I kept her on because she was such good company and always made me laugh. I liked her straightforward way of talking. One day she said to me, 'Of course, I don't know, ma'am,' she said, 'but Miss Florence's posture is just like a married woman.' Poor Miss Florence did have trouble \u2013 she fell in love with a gentleman from the hair salon who was very polite. I spoke to him, and they had a very nice wedding and settled down happily. Miss Florence was a good girl, but she was easily attracted to polite men.\" [1618] \"Did she do anything criminal?\" asked Yuan Yuan. \"I mean, the parlor maid.\" [1619] \"No, really,\" said Miss Marple. \"She married a Baptist minister, and they had three children.\" [1620] \"Just like me,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"Although so far, I only have Edward and Susan.\" [1621] After a moment, she added, \"Who are you thinking of, Aunt Jane?\" [1622] \"Many people, dear, many people,\" Miss Marple said evasively. [1623] \"In St. Mary Mead?\" [1624] \"Mostly, I suppose... I thought of Nurse Eaton \u2013 a truly remarkable, kind woman. She took care of an elderly lady who seemed to really like her. Then the lady died. And then another lady, and she died too. It turned out she had been administering morphine. In the kindest way possible, it was truly horrifying, that the woman didn't even feel she had done wrong. 'They weren't going to live long anyway,' she said. One had cancer, quite painful.\" [1625] \"You mean \u2013 it was a merciful murder?\" [1626] \"No, no. They left her money in their wills. She did it for the money, you know...\"\n[1627] \"Then there was the young man on the liner\u2014Pussycat's nephew from the paper shop. He brought home things he had stolen, said he bought them abroad, and she believed him. Then the police came and questioned him, and he pinned it all on her, so she couldn't get out of it... He wasn't a good man\u2014but he was good-looking, and two women fell in love with him. He spent a lot of money on one of them.\" [1628] \"I think that was the dirtiest one,\" said Round Round. [1629] \"Yes, dear. And then there was Mrs. Crewe from the wool shop, who doted on her son and spoiled him rotten. He got mixed up with a bad crowd. Do you remember Joan Crowfoot, Round Round?\" [1630] \"No, I don't.\" [1631] \"I think you met her when I took you around, always with a cigarette or a pipe in her mouth, striding around. There was a bank robbery, and Joan Crowfoot was there at the time. She knocked the man down and took his revolver. The judge commended her bravery. [1632] Round listened intently, as if committing everything to memory. [1633] \"And?\" she asked. [1634] \"That summer, there was a girl from Saint-Jean-de-Collins \u2013 such a quiet girl \u2013 not quiet in the sense of being silent, but everyone liked her, but not very well. We heard her husband was a forger, which made her feel isolated by people. It changed her, you know, depression can change a person. [1635] \"Do you remember any British colonels who served in India, dear?\" [1636] \"Of course, dear. There's a Major Vance at Rajes, and a Colonel Wright in Shimla. They're fine. But I do remember Mr. Hodgson, who went on a long journey and married a young woman who could have been his daughter. I don't know where she came from \u2013 except what she told him.\" [1637] \"And she wasn't telling the truth?\" [1638] \"No, dear, she definitely wasn't.\" [1639] \"Not bad,\" Round nodded, counting people on her fingers, \"we have the whole-hearted Dorla, the dashing Patrick, Mrs. Sweetnam, Edmund, Philippa Hemmes, Colonel Eastbrook and his wife \u2013 if I may say so, your opinion about Dorla is spot on. But she had no reason to murder Letitia Blacklock.\" [1640] \"There may be something Letitia Blacklock knew but didn't want others to know.\" [1641] \"Oh, dear, just the usual old stuff? That must be ancient history.\" [1642] \"Perhaps not. You see, Round Round, you're not the type to care what people think of you.\" [1643] \"I see what you mean,\" Round Round said suddenly. \"When you've been through a lot of hard times, like a lost cat shaking with cold, and then you find a home, a warm hand to stroke you, and people call you a pretty kitty and care for you wholeheartedly\u2026 to keep all that, you'd do anything. Well, I must say, you've shown me all sorts of people.\" [1644] \"But you don't see them clearly.\" Miss Marple said gently. [1645] \"Do I miss anything? Julia? Julia, the lovely Julia is so odd.\" [1646] \"Three shillings and sixpence,\" the waitress said, coming out of the shadows. [1647] \"And besides,\" she went on, her chest heaving under the \"bluebird\" on her uniform, \"I want to know, Mrs. Harmon, why you say I'm peculiar. I have a cousin who's one of the 'peculiar ones', but I've always been a Church of England myself, and retired Hopkins can tell you that.\" [1648] \"I'm really sorry,\" round round said. \"I was just quoting a song. I didn't mean you. I didn't know your name was Julia.\" [1649] \"Well, that's quite a coincidence,\" the waitress said, her attitude softening. \"I believe you didn't mean to offend, but hearing my name\u2026 well, it's natural, isn't it? If you think someone is talking about you, it's human nature to listen. Thank you.\" [1650] She took the tip and left. [1651] \"But it must be,\" Miss Marple murmured, \"it can't be like this. It doesn't make sense -\"\n[1652] \"Auntie!\" said Roundhay. [1653] Miss Marple sighed and smiled brightly. [1654] \"Nothing, dear,\" she said. [1655] \"Do you think you know who the murderer is?\" asked Roundhay. \"Who is it?\" [1656] \"I haven't the slightest idea,\" replied Miss Marple. \"I suddenly had an idea\u2014but it disappeared. I wish I knew. Time is so short, so very short.\" [1657] \"What do you mean by short?\" [1658] \"The Scottish old lady could die at any moment.\" [1659] Yuan Yuan's eyes widened. \"So you really believe in Pip and Emma, then? You think they did it\u2014and they'll do it again?\" [1660] \"They'll certainly do it again,\" said Miss Marple almost absent-mindedly. \"Once you've made up your mind to kill someone, you don't give up because you've failed the first time. Especially when you're sure you haven't been suspected.\" [1661] \"But if it's Pip and Emma,\" said Round Round, \"there are only two possibilities. It must be Patrick and Julia. They're brother and sister, and their ages fit.\" [1662] \"It's not so simple, my dear. There are all sorts of possibilities and combinations. There's Pip's wife\u2014if he's married\u2014or Emma's husband. There's their mother\u2014even if she can't inherit directly, she's interested. If Miss Blacklock hasn't seen her for thirty years, she may not recognize her now. Older women all look alike. You remember Mrs. Waters, who collected her own pension and Mrs. Butler's as well, even though Mrs. Butler had been dead for years. And Miss Blacklock is myopic. Have you noticed how she looks at people? Then there's their father, who's obviously a bad lot.\" [1663] \"Yes, but he's a foreigner.\" [1664] \"That's true, but there's no reason to believe that he speaks English with an accent or that he gestures wildly when he talks. I dare say he's playing the part of\u2014a British army officer serving in India, and doing it just as well as anyone else.\" [1665] \"Is that your idea?\" [1666] \"No, not really, dear. I just thought that a large sum of money is in danger, a large sum indeed. I know only too well that people will do terrible things for a large sum of money.\" [1667] \"I think they would, but it wouldn't benefit them, would it? There would be repercussions?\" [1668] \"Yes\u2014but they usually don't think that way.\" [1669] \"I can understand that.\" The Roundhead suddenly smiled, a sweet, crooked smile. \"Everyone feels differently about money\u2026 even me. I've been thinking, 'You'll get that money, and you'll use it for all sorts of good things. Make plans\u2026 provide a home for abandoned children. Send hard-working women to a foreign country to rest and relax after a lifetime of toil\u2026'\"\n[1670] Her expression turned somber, and her eyes suddenly looked sad and melancholy. [1671] \"I know what you're thinking,\" she said to Miss Marple, \"you think I would be the worst kind of person because I have children. If it's just for selfish reasons that you want that money, you would feel ashamed. But once you pretend that you're using the money for good deeds, you can convince yourself that maybe killing isn't such a big deal...\"\n[1672] Then her eyes brightened again. [1673] \"But I can't do it,\" she said. \"I can't bring myself to do it. Not even to old people, sick people, or people who have done terrible things in the world. Even if it's someone who is cheating or \u2013 or a real beast, it's not okay.\" [1674] She blew gently on the fly. It moved its legs and then wobbled away. [1675] \"Cheer up, dear Aunt Jane,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"I would never kill anyone.\" [1676] Chapter 14: Looking Back\n[1677] After a night on the train, Inspector Cordiner alighted at a small station in the Scottish Highlands. [1678] For a moment, he was surprised that the wealthy Mrs. Goddard \u2014 a disabled woman \u2014 had chosen to live in the remote Scottish home instead of the fashionable square in London, the Hampshire estate, or the villa in the south of France. She must have cut herself off from many friends and entertainment. This must be a lonely life \u2014 or was she so sick that she couldn't notice or care about her surroundings anymore? [1679] A car waited to pick him up, an old-fashioned, spacious Daimler driven by an elderly driver. It was a sunny morning, and during the twenty-mile drive, the inspector enjoyed himself despite being surprised by this choice for seclusion. A casual remark opened the driver's floodgates, giving him a rough understanding of the reason behind the decision. [1680] \"It's her home before she got married. Oh, she's the last one of the family. She was happier here with Mr. Goddard than anywhere else, even though he couldn't often get away from London. But whenever he came, they were like two children. Happy.\" [1681] As the ancient mansion's gray walls came into view, Inspector Corrado felt as though time had reversed. An elderly male butler greeted him, and after freshening up, the inspector was led to a room with a roaring fireplace, where he had breakfast. [1682] After breakfast, a middle-aged nurse dressed in a nurse's uniform entered the room, introducing herself as McLean Nurse. [1683] \"My patient is ready to see you, Inspector Corrado. She's been looking forward to meeting you.\u201d\n[1684] \"I'll do my best not to upset her,\" Corrado promised. [1685] \"I should warn you about what to expect. You'll find Mrs. Goddard to be perfectly normal. She'll talk and enjoy talking, and then - suddenly - her energy will collapse. At that point, please leave immediately and call me. You'll see that she's mostly kept alive by morphine. Most of the time, she's in a half-conscious state. I've given her a shot of stimulant to stay awake for your visit. As the stimulant wears off, she'll return to her semi-comatose state.\" [1686] \"I understand, Nurse MacLane. Would it be appropriate for me to ask about Mrs. Goddard's exact health condition?\" [1687] \"Well, Mr. Corrado, she is a dying woman. Her life expectancy is only a few weeks. If you find it strange that she has not passed away years ago, you may be right, but that is the truth. What has kept Mrs. Goddard alive is her intense desire and love for life. For someone who has suffered for years and has not left her home in fifteen years, it may seem unusual, but it is true. Mrs. Goddard has never been a robust woman, yet her will to survive has been remarkable. She is still a very charming woman, you will find.\" [1688] Corrado was led into a large bedroom with a fire burning, where an elderly woman lay on a bed with a canopy. Although she was only a few years older than Letitia Blacklock, her frail body made her look