m? This is Ridestone.\" [2245] \"Yes, sir.\" [2246] \"I've gone through your report. When you talked to Felicia Hammers, she positively claimed that she hadn't seen her husband since he ran away from the army, is that right?\" [2247] \"Yes, sir. She said it very positively. But I think she's not telling the truth.\" [2248] \"I agree with your opinion. Do you remember the case ten days ago? A man was hit by a large truck and then sent to Milchester General Hospital, resulting in a concussion and pelvic fracture, do you remember?\" [2249] \"The one who saved the child from under the wheel and got hurt himself?\" [2250] \"That's the one. He had no identification and no one came to claim him. It looks like he was wanted by the police. He never regained consciousness and died last night. But his identity was established. He was a deserter named Ronald Hammers, who served as a captain in South Lom County.\" [2251] \"Phyllis Hyde's husband?\" [2252] \"Yes. He had an old ticket to Chipping Craighorne and, by the way, a lot of money.\" [2253] \"So he did get money from his wife. I always thought he was the one talking to Phyllis in the gazebo. Of course, she denied it. Sir, the accident happened before\u2014\"\n[2254] Ridestad interrupted him:\n[2255] \"Yes. He was admitted to Milchester General Hospital on the 28th, and the robbery at the small farm took place on the 29th. This rules out any possibility of his being involved in the incident. However, his wife, of course, doesn't know about the accident. She's keeping quiet, which is natural, as he is her husband.\" [2256] \"Truly a heroic act of bravery, isn't it, sir?\" Corradino said slowly. [2257] \"Saving a child from under the wheels? Yeah, that's something. Don't think Hammers fled from the army out of cowardice. But that's all in the past now. For a man who ruined his own reputation, it's a fitting end.\" [2258] \"I am happy for her,\" said the Inspector, \"and for their son as well.\" [2259] \"Yes, he need not feel too ashamed of his father. The young woman can remarry again.\" [2260] Kordok slowly said, \"I was also thinking about this, Director... this shows... possibilities.\" [2261] \"Since you were on the scene, it would be best for you to deliver the news.\u201d\n[2262] \"I will, Chief. I'll go right away. Perhaps it would be best to wait until she returns to the small enclosure. The news might be quite shocking, and besides, I'd like to talk to someone else first.\" [2263] ** ** **\n[2264] [1] Charlotte's first letter is C.\n[2265] Chapter Nineteen: Recap of the Case\n[2266] 1\n[2267] \"I'll get a lamp and put it on the side for you, and then I'll leave,\" said Round Round. \"It's quite dark here. I think a storm is coming.\" [2268] She picked up the small reading lamp and put it on the other side of the table, so that the light would shine on Miss Marple as she knitted her sweater. She was sitting in a large high-backed chair. [2269] The wire was run across the table, and the cat leaped up onto the table and began biting and clawing at the wire. [2270] \"Don't move, Tigris, don't\u2026 It's terrible. Look, it's almost bitten through the wire, it's all broken. Don't you know? You silly little cat, you'll get electrocuted if you do that.\" [2271] \"Thank you, dear,\" said Miss Maple, reaching out to turn on the light. [2272] \"Not that one. You have to press the small switch in the middle of the wire. Wait a moment, I'll take these flowers away so they don't get in the way.\" [2273] She picked up a bottle of Christmas roses from the other end of the table. Tiggy wagged his tail and suddenly extended a mischievous paw, scratching Yuan Yuan's arm. She splashed some water from the vase onto the wires that Tiggy had bitten and onto Tiggy himself, who growled angrily and jumped off the table. [2274] Miss Maple pressed the small pear-shaped switch. A spark burst from the spot where the wires had been bitten and wet with water. [2275] \"Oh, dear,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"The fuse has blown. Now I guess all the lights should be off. \" She tried each switch. \"Yes, they're all off. So, all the lines are connected to this small device, how stupid. And it's burned a spot on the table. It's all Tiggy's fault, the mischievous one. Auntie Jane, what's wrong? Are you scared?\" [2276] \"No, dear. I just happened to notice something I should have seen before\u2026\"\n[2277] \"I'll go change the fuse and then bring the table lamp from Julian's study.\" [2278] \"No, dear, don't bother. You'll miss your bus. I don't need the light anymore, I just want to sit here and think. Go on, dear, or you'll miss your bus.\" [2279] After Miss Marple left, she sat quietly for a couple of minutes. The air in the room was heavy and damp, indicating an approaching storm. [2280] Miss Marple moved a piece of paper in front of her. [2281] She wrote down: \"Lamp?\" and drew a thick line underneath. [2282] After a while, she wrote down another word. [2283] Her pen glided across the paper, scribbling down brief, cryptic phrases\u2026\n[2284] 2\n[2285] The drawing room at Grasmere Hall had low ceilings and stained-glass windows that cast a dim light. Miss Quincannon and Miss Muggleton were arguing inside. [2286] \"Your problem, Mulgrew,\" said Miss Quincannon, \"is that you won't try.\" [2287] \"I tell you, Quincannon, I don't remember anything.\" [2288] \"Well, listen here, Amy Mulgrew, we need to do some constructive thinking. So far, we haven't made any progress in the investigation. I was wrong about the door. After all, you didn't hold it open for the killer. You're innocent, Mulgrew!\" [2289] Miss Mulgrew smiled faintly. [2290] \"It's fortunate that we have a cleaning woman who knows how to keep quiet, Chipping Chilcote. I usually appreciate that, but this time, she's put us in a bad position. Everyone knows that the second door in the living room was used, and we didn't know until yesterday...\"\n[2291] \"I'm still not quite clear \u2014\"\n[2292] \"It's simple. Our original assumption was correct: you couldn't have kept the door open while holding a flashlight and a revolver in your hand. We kept the revolver and the flashlight, but we should have omitted the revolver. We made a mistake.\" [2293] \"But he did have a revolver,\" said Miss Mulgrew. \"I saw it on the ground next to him.\" [2294] \"He didn't use it when he was alive \u2014\"\n[2295] \"Then who fired the shot?\" [2296] \"We're looking for that person. But whoever fired the shot, the same person put two poisoned aspirins on Miss Blacklock's bedside table, which killed poor Dora Bunner. And it couldn't have been Rudi Schlesinger, because he was dead as a doornail. It was the person in the living room on the night of the robbery, and that person may have been at the birthday party. The only one who wasn't there was Mrs. Harmon.\" [2297] \"You think someone put poisoned aspirins there on the night of the birthday party?\" [2298] \"Why not?\" [2299] \"But how could they do that?\" [2300] \"Well, we all went to the bathroom, didn't we?\" said Miss Inchcliff gruffly. \"I washed my hands because the cake was so sticky. And Miss Eastbrooke was powdering Miss Blacklock's face in the bathroom, wasn't she?\" [2301] \"Chin! You think it was her? \u2014\"\n[2302] \"I don't know. If she did, it would be too obvious. If you wanted to put the pills there, I don't think you'd want someone to see you in the bathroom, would you? There are plenty of other opportunities.\" [2303] \"The men didn't go upstairs.\" [2304] \"There's the back staircase. Besides, if a man left the house, you wouldn't follow him around to make sure he really went where you did, would you? That would be undignified! Anyway, don't argue with me, Miss Mulgrew. I want to start over from the first murder of Letty Blacklock. Now, first of all, remember the facts, because it all depends on you.\" [2305] Miss Mulgrew looked tense. [2306] \"Oh, dear, Chippy, you know I'm all mixed up about all this! -\"\n[2307] \"The problem is not with your brain, or with the gray cells you call your brain. The problem is with your eyes; the problem is what you saw at the time. -\"\n[2308] \"But I saw nothing. -\"\n[2309] \"As I said, your trouble is, Murgatroyd, you don't try hard enough. Now, here's what happened that night: whoever went to kill Letty Blacklock that night was in the house. He - I say he, because it's more convenient to say, but there's no reason to assume it was a man and not a woman, except that men are generally scoundrels - anyway, he oiled the door from the living room to the outside, which should have been nailed shut. Don't ask me when he did it, because that would muddy the waters. In fact, if I had to pick a time, I could walk into any pub in Chipping Clegg-horn and do anything I wanted in about half an hour without anyone noticing. Find out where the staff is, when the owner goes out, where exactly he's going, and for how long. Now, let's think, he oiled the second door so that it wouldn't make a sound when opened. The plan was: lights out, door A - the front door - opened with a bang. Flash a flashlight and say the word \"robbery\". Meanwhile, while we were all gaping, X - that's the best way to call him - slipped quietly from door B into the hallway, came up behind the Swiss idiot, and shot Letty Blacklock twice before shooting the Swiss. Then he dropped the gun. So only someone like you, who doesn't like to think, would think that was evidence of the Swiss firing the gun. Then he quickly made his way back to the living room while we were looking for a lighter. Understand? -\"\n[2310] \"Yes, yes, but who was it? -\"\n[2311] \"Well, if you don't know, Muggleton, then nobody does!\" [2312] \"Me?\" Muggleton exclaimed in surprise. \"I don't know. I really don't, Quinn!\" [2313] \"Use that thing you call a brain. First, where was everyone when the lights went out?\" [2314] \"I don't know.\" [2315] \"No, you do. You're just muddled, Muggleton. You know where you were yourself, don't you? You were behind the door.\" [2316] \"Yes, yes, I was behind the door. I bumped my toe when the door opened.\" [2317] \"Why can't you go see a proper foot doctor, instead of messing around with it? You're going to get gangrene one of these days. So, you were behind the door, I was standing by the fireplace, and I was just about to have a drink. Letty Blacklock was at the table in the arcade, reaching for a cigarette. Patrick went through the arcade to the small sitting room to get the drink Letty Blacklock had left there. Agreed?\" [2318] \"Yes, yes. I remember all that.\" [2319] \"Good. Now, someone was following Patrick through the small sitting room, or was about to go with him, a man. The question is, I can't remember whether it was Eastbrooke or Edmund Swetnam. Do you remember?\" [2320] \"No, I don't.\" [2321] \"You just can't remember! Someone else went to the small parlor, it was Philippa Hemmes. I remember this very well because I remember noticing how beautiful her straight back was, and I thought to myself: 'The girl would look good riding a horse.' I was looking at her, thinking about that. She walked to the fireplace in the small parlor, I don't know what she went there for, because just then the lights went out. [2322] \"That's how everyone was positioned: Patrick Simmons was in the living room, Philippa Hemmes was there, and either Eastbrook Colonel or Edmund Swetnam - but I don't know who. Now, Muggleton, pay attention, the most likely culprit is one of these three. Anyone who wanted to get out of the door from a distance would have to be in a convenient position to act as soon as the lights went out. So I say the most likely culprit is one of these three. If that's the case, Muggleton, you're out of luck!\" [2323] It was clear that Miss Muggleton's face lit up. [2324] \"On the other hand,\" Miss Chinchcliffe continued, \"it may not be any of the three. That's where you come in, Muggleton.\" [2325] \"But how could I know the situation at the time?\" [2326] \"I've already said that if you don't know, then no one does.\" [2327] \"But I don't know! I really don't know! I couldn't see anything!\" [2328] \"Oh, no, you could see. You were the only one who could see. You were standing behind the door, and you couldn't look at the flashlight because the door was between you and the flashlight. You were facing the other way, in the same direction as the flashlight. The rest of us were blinded by the flashlight, but you weren't.\" [2329] \"Yes, yes, maybe so, but I couldn't see anything, the flashlight was flashing around.\" [2330] \"What did it illuminate? Was it shining on everyone's faces? On the table? On the chairs?\" [2331] \"Yes, yes, that's right... Miss Bonner, she had her mouth wide open, her eyes bulging out, blinking in terror.\" [2332] \"That's right! It was hard to get you to use your gray cells, Miss Quincannon. So, what happened next?\" [2333] \"But I didn't see anything else, really.\" [2334] \"So, you saw an empty room? With no one standing or sitting?\" [2335] \"No, of course not. Miss Bonner had her eyes wide open, and Mrs. Harmon was sitting on the arm of a chair, with her eyes closed tightly and her hands covering her face \u2013 like a child.\" [2336] \"Very well, these are Mrs. Harmon and Miss Bonner. Do you still not understand what I'm getting at? The difficult part is that I don't want to put my thoughts into your head. But once we eliminate the people you have seen, we can get to the point, which is whether there are any people you haven't seen. Understood? Besides the table, chair, chrysanthemums, and so on, there are still some people left: Julia Simmons, Mrs. Swetnam, Mrs. Eastbrook \u2013 either Colonel Eastbrook or Mr. Edmund Swetnam, Miss Dora Bonner, and Round-harmon. Cross them off one by one. Now, think, Mr. Mulgrew, think carefully, are there any who were not present at the time?\" [2337] A branch hung onto the open window, and Miss Muggleton jumped slightly in fright. She closed her eyes and muttered to herself\u2026\n[2338] \"The flowers on the table... the big armchair... the flashlight's beam hasn't reached you yet, Mrs. Harmon, yes...\". [2339] The telephone rang urgently. Miss Quincy walked up to the telephone. [2340] \"Hello, yes. Police station?\" [2341] Miss Muggeridge closed her eyes tightly and relived the scene from the night of the 29th. Flashlight, slowly scanning... a group of people... the window... the sofa... Dora Bonner... the walls... the table with the lamp... the arcade... the revolver suddenly firing...\n[2342] \"...that's quite unusual!\" said Miss Muggeridge. [2343] \"What?\" Miss Chinchcliffe shouted into the phone, angry. \"Has it been there since this morning? When?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAnd Mr. Edmund Swetnam, who disliked the Colonel, spoke up: \"The fact is, we're all under suspicion.\" [1824] \"But what's there to suspect?\" Mrs. Eastbrook asked again. [1825] \"Never mind, kitty,\" her husband said. [1826] \"On a pretext,\" Edmund said, \"the purpose is to catch the murderer on the spot.\" [1827] \"Oh, no, please don't say that, Mr. Sweetnam,\" Dora Bunner cried, \"I don't believe anyone here would want to kill dear, dear Letty.\" [1828] There was an awkward silence. Edmund's face turned red, and he whispered, \"It was just a joke.\" Philippa raised her voice and suggested going to listen to the six o'clock news, and everyone agreed eagerly. [1829] Patrick whispered to Julia, \"We need Mrs. Harmon here. She would certainly yell, 'But I think someone is still looking for an opportunity to kill Miss Blacklock!'\" [1830] \"I'm glad she and that Miss Maple didn't come,\" Julia said, \"That old woman is the type who likes to pry everywhere. I think her mind is full of all sorts of strange ideas. Very Victorian, I would say.\" [1831] While listening to the news, the conversation quickly turned to the horrors of nuclear war. Colonel Eastbrook claimed that the real threat to civilization was undoubtedly the Russians, while Edmund said he had some charming Russian friends - everyone reacted coldly to his statement. [1832] The guests thanked the hostess again, and the party came to an end. [1833] \"Did you have a good time, Bonnie?\" Miss Blacklock asked after the last guest had left. [1834] \"Yes, I did. But my head is aching. I think it's from excitement.\" [1835] \"It's the cake,\" Patrick said, \"I feel a bit unwell. And you've been nibbling on chocolate all morning.\" [1836] \"I think I'll lie down,\" Miss Bunns said. \"I'll take two aspirin, and then try to get a good night's sleep.\" [1837] \"That's a good idea,\" Miss Blacklock said. [1838] Miss Bunns went upstairs. [1839] \"Shall I put the ducks away for you, Aunt Letty?\" [1840] Miss Blacklock looked at Patrick sternly. [1841] \"If you promise to lock the door.\" [1842] \"I will. I swear I will.\" [1843] \"Have a sherry, Aunt Letty,\" said Julia. \"As my old nurse used to say, 'It will settle your stomach.' It's rather an unpalatable suggestion, but it's uncannily appropriate at the moment.\" [1844] \"Well, I dare say it may be a good thing. People are not used to greasy things nowadays. Ah, Bonnie, you did startle me. What's the matter?\" [1845] \"I can't find my aspirin,\" said Miss Bonner, looking disconsolate. [1846] \"Then take some of mine, on my bedside table.\" [1847] \"I have one on my dressing table too,\" said Felicia. [1848] \"Thank you \u2013 very much. If I can't find it \u2013 I remember where I put it, a new bottle. Where on earth did I put it?\" [1849] \"There's a pile of them in the bedroom,\" Julia said impatiently. \"There's plenty of aspirin in the house.\" [1850] \"I'm so careless with my things, it's so frustrating,\" Miss Bonner said, and went back upstairs. [1851] \"Poor old Bonnie,\" Julia adjusted her glasses, \"do you think we ought to give her sherry?\" [1852] \"No, I don't think so,\" said Miss Blacklock, \"she's too excited today, and it would do her no good. I'm afraid she'll feel worse tomorrow. But I do think she's had a nice time today.\" [1853] \"She's had a lovely time,\" said Phyllis. [1854] \"Let's give Midge a glass of sherry,\" Julia suggested, and when she heard Patrick come in she called, \"Pat, ask Midge to come here.\" [1855] Midge came in and Julia poured her a glass of sherry. [1856] \"Cheers to the best chef in the world,\" said Patrick. [1857] Micki felt satisfied \u2013 but felt she should protest. [1858] \"It's not like that. I'm actually not a chef. In my country, I do brain work.\" [1859] \"That's a waste,\" said Patrick, \"How can brain work compare to a master chef who cooks up delicious meals?\" [1860] \"Oh \u2013 I told you I didn't like \u2013\"\n[1861] \"I don't care what you like, my dear,\" Patrick said, \"I named it that. Let's toast to delicious death, and screw the aftertaste!\" [1862] 3\n[1863] \"Philippa, my dear, I want to talk to you.\" [1864] \"Oh, Miss Blacklock?\" [1865] Philippa raised her head slightly in surprise. [1866] \"What's bothering you, right?\" [1867] \"Bothering?\" [1868] \"I've noticed you've been looking worried lately. Anything wrong?\" [1869] \"Oh, no, Miss Blacklock. Why must there be something wrong?\" [1870] \"Hmm\u2014I was wondering\u2026I suppose maybe you and Patrick\u2014\"\n[1871] \"Patrick?\" Flickering with surprise. [1872] \"So it's not that, then. If I'm wrong, please forgive me. But you two are always together. Even though Patrick is my cousin, I don't think he would make a satisfactory husband. At least, not for a while.\" [1873] Flickering's face was stiff with no expression. [1874] \"I won't marry again.\" she said. [1875] \"Ah, not yet, my dear child, you're still young. But we don't need to discuss this. Is there any other trouble? You're not worried about\u2014like, money, for example?\" [1876] \"No, I'm fine.\" [1877] \"I know you sometimes worry about the children's education, so I wanted to tell you something. This afternoon, I drove to Milchester to see my lawyer, Mr. Beddingfield. Things haven't been fully settled yet, and I want to make a new will \u2013 just in case of unforeseen circumstances. Besides the inheritance for Bonnie, everything else goes to you, Philippa.\" [1878] \"What?\" Philippa spun around, her eyes wide with shock. She looked utterly bewildered, even panicked. [1879] \"But I don't want \u2013 I really don't want \u2013 oh, I'd rather not \u2013 but why, why leave it to me?\" [1880] \"Perhaps,\" Miss Blacklock said in a strange voice, \"because there's no one else.\" [1881] \"But there's Patrick and Julia.\" [1882] \"Yes, there is Patrick and Julia.\" Miss Blacklock's odd tone remained unchanged. [1883] \"They're your relatives.\" [1884] \"Distant relatives. They have no right to make demands on me.\" [1885] \"I - I don't - I don't know what you're thinking... Oh, I don't want it.\" [1886] Her gaze, fixed on Miss Blacklock, was less one of gratitude than of vehement protest, and her manner was almost one of terror. [1887] \"I know what I'm doing, Philippa. I've fallen in love with you - and the boy - and if I were to die now, you wouldn't get much - but in a few weeks, things might be different.\" [1888] She fixed Philippa with a stare. [1889] \"But you won't die!\" Philippa protested. [1890] \"If I take the proper measures, I won't.\" [1891] \"Precautions?\" [1892] \"Yes, think it over\u2014don't worry about it.\" [1893] She suddenly left the room, and Philpotts heard her talking to Julia in the hall. [1894] After a while, Julia entered the drawing room. Her eyes were cold. [1895] \"You've certainly got a way with you, haven't you, Philpotts? I see you're one of those dark horses\u2014a black horse.\" [1896] \"So you heard\u2014\"\n[1897] \"Yes, I heard. I'd rather admit that I intentionally listened.\" (Yes, I heard. I'd rather admit that I intentionally listened.) [1898] \"What do you mean?\" (What do you mean?) [1899] \"Our Letty isn't a fool... but, anyway, you did quite well, Philippa. You had the upper hand, didn't you?\" (But, anyway, you did quite well, Philippa. You had the upper hand, didn't you?) [1900] \"Oh, Julia\u2014I didn't mean to\u2014I never intended to\u2014\" (Oh, Julia\u2014I didn't mean to\u2014I never intended to\u2014)\n[1901] \"Didn't you? You certainly did. You're dissatisfied with everything, aren't you? Starving for money. But remember this\u2014if anyone offs Aunt Letty, you're the prime suspect.\" [1902] \"But I wouldn't. If\u2014if I had her killed before she could wait, that would be stupid\u2014\"\n[1903] \"So you know that old bat in Scotland is on her deathbed? I was wondering\u2026 Philly, now I'm starting to believe you're quite a formidable opponent.\" [1904] \"I don't want to interfere with you and Patrick.\" [1905] \"Don't you, my dear? I'm sorry\u2014but I don't believe you.\" [1906] Chapter 16: The Inspector Returns\n[1907] Inspector Corrado takes the night train back, but he sleeps poorly. He keeps dreaming, dreams that are more like nightmares. Over and over again, he runs through the dim corridors of a castle, trying to get somewhere, or to stop something. Finally, he dreams that he wakes up, a great sense of relief washing over him. Then, his compartment door slides open, and Letitia Blacklock sticks her bloodied head in, looking at him reproachfully and saying, \"Why didn't you save me? You could have done it if you'd tried.\" [1908] This time, he really wakes up. [1909] Thankfully, Inspector Corrado finally arrives in Milchester. He goes straight to the station and reports to Ridestead, who listens attentively. [1910] \"This trip didn't advance the case,\" he says, \"but it did confirm what Miss Blacklock told you. Pip and Emma\u2014well, let me think.\" [1911] \"Patrick and Julia Simmons' ages match up, Inspector. Assuming we can confirm that Miss Blacklock never saw them as adults \u2013\u201d\n[1912] Reddlesdale smiles slightly and says, \"Our ally Miss Marple has already confirmed that for us. In fact, Miss Blacklock hadn't seen them until two months ago.\" [1913] \"Well, then, it seems clear, Inspector \u2013\u201d\n[1914] \"It's not that simple, Corradino. We've been checking, and based on the information we have, Patrick and Julia seem to have no connection to the case. Patrick's naval records are genuine \u2013 he's performed well, with no signs of disobeying orders. We've also checked with Cannes, and a woman who claims to be Mrs. Simmons angrily says her son and daughter are, of course, living with her cousin Letitia Blacklock in Chipping Cleghorn.\" [1915] \"But is that Mrs. Simmons really the true Mrs. Simmons?\" [1916] \"She's been called Mrs. Simmons for a long time, I can only say that,\" Ridestone replied dryly. [1917] \"That seems clear enough. It's just\u2014the two match. The ages match, and Miss Blacklock herself doesn't recognize them. If you want to find Pip and Emma, there they are.\" [1918] The commissioner nodded thoughtfully and pushed a sheet of paper towards Corradino. [1919] \"This is some of the information we've gathered on Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [1920] The inspector raised his eyebrows as he read. [1921] \"Very interesting,\" he said. \"She kept the old man in the dark about it, didn't she? But I don't see how it's relevant to the case.\" [1922] \"On the surface, it doesn't seem to be.\" [1923] \"But this one does pertain to Mrs. Hyde.\" [1924] Corrigan raised his eyebrows again. [1925] \"I think I'll have to talk to this lady again,\" he said. \"Do you think this information could be relevant to the case?\" [1926] \"I think it might be. Of course, it could also be a waste of time\u2026\"\n[1927] The two fell silent for a moment. [1928] \"Any progress from Fletcher, Chief?\" [1929] \"Fletcher is very active. After obtaining Miss Blacklock's consent, he conducted a routine search of the mansion, but found nothing significant. Then he tried to find out who had the opportunity to oil the door and who was in the mansion when the foreign girl was out. The situation is more complicated than we thought, as she seems to go out for a walk in the afternoon. Usually to the village, to have a cup of coffee at 'The Bluebird.' So, when Miss Blacklock and Miss Bonner go out \u2013 which is usually in the afternoon \u2013 to pick blackberries, it's open for anyone to come in.\" [1930] \"And the door was never locked, then?\" [1931] \"It used to be. But I imagine it would be locked now.\" [1932] \"What results did Fletcher get? Who went into the house when it was empty?\" [1933] \"Actually, they all did.\" [1934] Ridestad looked at the page in front of him. [1935] \"Miss Mulgrew took a hen to hatch eggs. This sounds a bit redundant, but that's what she said. She was very flustered and contradictory in her statements. But Fletcher believes it was due to her personality rather than guilt.\" [1936] \"Maybe so,\" Corradino admitted. \"She was flustered.\" [1937] \"Next was Mrs. Sweetnam, who came to pick up the beef that Miss Blacklock had left on the kitchen table for her. Because Miss Blacklock drove to Milchester that day, and every time she went there, she always brought her some beef. Does that mean anything to you?\" [1938] Corradino pondered. [1939] \"Why didn't Miss Blacklock leave the beef at Mrs. Sweetnam's house on her way back from Milchester?\" [1940] \"I don't know, but she didn't. Mrs. Sweetnam said that Miss Blacklock always left the beef on the kitchen table, and she, Mrs. Sweetnam, liked to wait until Miss Blacklock wasn't around to take it, because Miss Blacklock could be quite rude sometimes.\" [1941] \"That does make sense. Who's next?\" [1942] \"Miss Inchcliff. She said she hadn't been there recently, but she actually had. Because Mickey saw her come out of the side door, and Mrs. Barton too - she's a local. Miss Inchcliff later admitted that she might have gone, but she forgot, didn't remember what she was doing, and said she probably just dropped by.\" [1943] \"That is quite strange.\" [1944] \"Obviously just like her behavior. Then there's Mrs. Eastbrook, who walks her dog on that path and stopped by to see if Blacklock had a knitting pattern she could borrow, but Blacklock wasn't there. She said she waited inside for a while.\" [1945] \"So that's what happened. Perhaps she was snooping around, or just oiling the door. And what about the Colonel?\" [1946] \"One day he went over with a book about India, which Miss Blacklock had expressed an interest in.\" [1947] \"She really did?\" [1948] \"She said she'd rather not look at it, but it didn't do any good.\" [1949] \"That's a fair point,\" said Cora Dedalus. \"If someone keeps pestering you to borrow something, you can't get rid of it!\" [1950] \"We don't know if Edmund Swetnam has been there. He was vague about it, saying he sometimes pops in on his way to do something for his mother, but he doesn't think it's been recently.\" [1951] \"After all, it's still too early to draw conclusions.\" [1952] \"Yes.\" [1953] \"Ridderdale smiled slightly and said, \"Miss Maple was very active. Fletcher reported that she went to 'Bluebird' House for coffee one morning, then to the Gravel Pits for sherry, and to the Little Paddocks for tea. She admired Mrs. Swetnam's garden and paid a visit to Colonel Eastbrook's house to admire his Indian antiques.\" [1954] \"She might be able to tell us if this Colonel Eastbrook is a real or a fake.\" [1955] \"She will find out, I agree - he seems to be all right. We will check with the British authorities in the Far East to confirm his identity.\" [1956] \"Meanwhile,\" Corrado interrupted him, \"do you think Miss Blacklock would agree to leave?\" [1957] \"Leave Chipping Cleghorn?\" [1958] \"Yes. Perhaps with the faithful Bunner in tow, to a place where no one knows her. Why doesn't she go to Scotland to stay with Bella Goddard? It's a remote place.\" [1959] \"Just stay there and wait for her to die? I don't think she would do that. I don't think any kind-hearted woman would like that suggestion.\" [1960] \"If it's about saving her life -\"\n[1961] \"Well, Kollarik, killing someone isn't as easy as you think.\u201d\n[1962] \"Isn't it, sir?\u201d\n[1963] \"Well\u2014in one sense, I agree, it's quite simple. There are plenty of ways of doing it, like using weed killer, or hitting her on the head when she comes out to let the chickens out, or hiding behind the hedge and throwing a pot at her. That's all quite simple. But to kill someone and not be suspected of it, that's not so easy. The murderer must realize he's being watched. The carefully planned scheme has failed, and our unknown murderer has to make other arrangements.\u201d\n[1964] \"I understand that, sir. But the murderer has to consider time. Mrs. Gowder is dying, and she might die any minute. That means the murderer can't wait.\u201d\n[1965] \"Quite.\u201d\n[1966] \"And another thing, Chief, the killer must know that we are investigating everyone.\" [1967] \"And that takes time,\" Laidesdale sighed. \"That means checking with the East, in India. Yes, it's a tedious and time-consuming job.\" [1968] \"So, that's another reason to hurry.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2860] \"I studied her, my dear,\" said Miss Marple. \"She's too self-important, so it does her good to do something for others. Of course, I flattered her, saying I believed she would have joined the resistance if she had stayed in her homeland, and she said, 'Yes, of course.' I said she had the right temperament for the job, brave and not afraid of danger, and could play a role. Some of it was true, and some of it was probably made up. She was thrilled!\" [2861] \"Excellent,\" Patrick commented. [2862] \"So, I convinced her to agree to play her part. I rehearsed her until she was perfect. Then I sent her upstairs to her room and told her to wait until Inspector Corcoran came to fetch her. For people who are easily excited, it's important not to act too soon.\" [2863] \"She did a great job,\" Julia said. [2864] \"I don't quite understand the logic,\" Round said, with an apologetic tone. [2865] \"The logic is a bit complicated \u2013 and quite risky. The idea is that Midge casually admits to having thought about blackmailing, but now she's afraid and willing to tell the truth. She saw Miss Blacklock with a revolver in her hand, coming up behind Rudi Scherz in the restaurant door. That's what she saw happen. The only danger now is that Charlotte Blacklock might figure out the plan, because there was a key in the lock, and Midge couldn't have seen anything. But my bet is that, being suddenly frightened, she wouldn't think of that. She would only believe that Midge saw her.\" [2866] \"But\u2014this is crucial\u2014when I heard this, I pretended to be skeptical, and then, as if I had run out of options, I accused someone who had not been suspected before. I accused Edmund\u2014\"\n[2867] \"And I played my role excellently,\" said Edmund, \"denying it all. The plan went according to plan. But the unexpected happened when Philippa, my dear, you stepped in and publicly admitted that you were 'Pip.' Neither the inspector nor I had thought that you were Pip. I was supposed to be Pip! This threw our plan off course, but the inspector came back with a vengeance, insinuating in a vicious and unassailable way that I wanted to marry a rich woman. Now his words have probably penetrated your subconscious, and someday they will cause irreparable trouble between us.\" [2868] \"Why is that necessary?\" ROUND asked. [2869] \"Isn't it? According to Charlotte Blacklock's point of view, this means that only Midge suspects and knows the truth. The police suspect others and consider Midge a fraud for the time being. But if Midge insists, they may believe her and take everything she says seriously. So, Midge must be silenced.\" [2870] \"Midge walked out of the kitchen, back to the kitchen\u2014exactly as I taught her,\" said Miss Marple, \"Miss Blacklock came out almost immediately. On the surface, Midge was alone in the kitchen. In reality, Fletcher was hiding behind the door of the pantry, and I was hiding in the broom closet, thankfully thin.\" [2871] Round looked at Miss Marple. [2872] \"What did you anticipate, Aunt Jane?\" [2873] \"There are two possibilities. One is that Charlotte will bribe Micky to keep quiet, and then Fletcher Inspector will be the witness to the transaction. Or, I think she will try to kill Micky.\" [2874] \"But she won't expect to escape, will she? She will be suspected immediately.\" [2875] \"Oh, my dear, she has lost her mind. She is just a frightened, desperate, and vicious mouse that bites anyone she sees. Think about what happened that day, Miss Chinchcliff and Miss Muggleton's scene. Miss Chinchcliff went to the police station, and when she came back, Miss Muggleton would explain that Letitia Blacklock was not in the living room that night. To make Miss Muggleton unable to speak, there was only a short time to act. There was no time for planning or staging a scene, only a cruel murder. She greeted the poor girl, then strangled her. Then she rushed home to change clothes and sat by the fireplace waiting for others to come in, as if she had never left.\" [2876] \"But why did you hide in the broom cupboard, Aunt Joan?\" Yuan Yuan asked. \"Why didn't you let Fletcher Inspector do it?\" [2877] \"We were safe together, my dear. Besides, I know I can imitate Dora Bunner's voice. That's what would break Charlotte Blacklock, if anything could.\" [2878] \"Indeed!\" said Yuan Yuan. [2879] \"Yes... she broke down.\" [2880] \"Yes...\"\n[2881] There was a long silence as everyone was lost in thought, until Julia broke the tension with a firm and light tone: \"It greatly changed Micky. She told me yesterday she got a job near Southampton. And she said -\" Julia mimicked Micky's accent perfectly, \"I'm going there, if they tell you to register at the police station because you're a foreigner. I'll tell them: 'Yes, I'll register. The police know me. I've helped the police! Without me, the police wouldn't have been able to arrest a very dangerous criminal. I risked my life because I'm brave, brave as a lion. I don't care about danger.' 'Micky,' they said to me, 'you're a heroine, you're amazing.' I said, 'Oh, it's nothing.'\" [2882] Julia stopped. [2883] \"She said a lot more,\" she added. [2884] \"I think,\" Edmund mused, \"that in the near future, Micky will help the police solve more cases!\" [2885] \"She became more mild-mannered towards me,\" said Felicity. \"In fact, she gave me the recipe for the deadly dish as a wedding gift. She said I must never reveal the recipe to Julia, because she ruined her omelette pan.\" [2886] \"Mrs. Lucas,\" Edmund said, \"fell in love with Phyllida because Phyllida and Julia inherited Mr. Goddle's millions. She gave us some silver clamps for asparagus as a wedding present. I'm glad she wasn't invited to the wedding!\" [2887] \"So they lived happily ever after,\" Patrick said, \"Edmund and Phyllida, and Julia and Patrick? He added, hastily. [2888] \"Don't you dare,\" Julia said, \"otherwise you won't have a happy life. Inspector Corradke's quick thinking to Edmund was more suitable for you. You're the kind of weak young man who likes rich ladies and is idle!\" [2889] \"Bad luck,\" Patrick said, \"I did so much for this girl.\" [2890] \"Almost got me in prison for murder,\" Julia said, \"that's what your forgetfulness almost brought about. I almost thought it was over when your sister's letter arrived that night. I couldn't see any way out.\" [2891] \"Well, since things have turned out this way,\" she added with a laugh, \"I think I'll go into acting.\" [2892] \"What? You too?\" groaned Patrick. [2893] \"Yes. I might go to Perth and see if I can get a place in the company with your sister. Then, when I've learned the ropes, I'll go into drama management, maybe even produce Edmund's play.\" [2894] \"I thought you were writing a novel,\" said Julian Harmon. [2895] \"Well, I was,\" answered Edmund. \"I started out writing a novel, quite good. Wrote a few pages about a bearded man who got up from bed and smelled, and the gray streets, and a horrible old lady with swollen legs and an evil young prostitute drooling. They went on and on about the state of the world, trying to figure out what it was all for. And then, suddenly, I started to wonder myself... a funny idea popped into my head... I wrote it down, and even designed a nice little set... all obvious stuff. But somehow, I got interested... and when I realized what I was doing, I had written a noisy three-act farce.\" [2896] \"What's it called?\" Patrick asked. \"The Butler's Observations?\" [2897] \"Well, it might be easier\u2026 actually, I named it The Elephant Really Forgets. Besides, the play has been accepted and is going to be performed!\" [2898] \"The Elephant Really Forgets,\" Rondy mumbled. \"I thought they had good memories.\" [2899] Julian Harmon exclaimed guiltily, \"Good heavens. I got so caught up in it. My sermon!\" [2900] \"It's a detective story,\" Rondy said. \"This time it's real life.\" [2901] \"You could preach 'Thou Shalt Not Kill,'\" Patrick suggested. [2902] \"No,\" said Julian Harmon calmly. \"I won't use that as my sermon.\" [2903] \"Yes,\" said Roundabout. \"You're right, Julian. I know there are better, happier sermons. She quoted, \"The earth is glad to see spring come, and hears the turtle's song\u2014I'm not reading it well, but you know what I mean. Although I can't figure out why it's a turtle. I don't think turtles have beautiful voices.\" [2904] \"The word 'turtle' in the Hebrew text,\" explained Julian Harmon the priest, \"doesn't convey the joyful meaning. It doesn't refer to a reptile, but to a dove. The original Hebrew is\u2014\"\n[2905] Roundabout interrupted him with a hug and said, \"I know one thing\u2014you think Xerxes in the Bible is Artaxerxes II, but only you and I know he's also Artaxerxes III.\" [2906] As usual, Julian didn't understand why his wife found that story particularly amusing. [2907] \"Tigris wants to help you,\" Round said. \"It should be proud of itself for showing us how the fuse of the light bulb broke.\" [2908] * * *\n[2909] [1] The word \"consult\" has two correct spellings: \"enquiries\" or \"inquiries,\" depending on personal preference. [2910] [2] The turtle is \"turtle,\" and the turtledove is \"turtle dove.\" [2911] Epilogue\n[2912] \"We should get some newspapers,\" Edmund said to Philippa on the day they returned from their honeymoon in Chipping Cleghorn, \"Let's go to Totman's.\" [2913] The slow-moving Mr. Totman greeted them warmly. [2914] \"It's good to see you back, sir, and madam.\" [2915] \"We want to order some newspapers.\" [2916] \"Of course, sir. How is your mother? Is she settled in Bournemouth?\" [2917] \"She likes it,\" Edmund said, although he wasn't entirely sure if that was true, but like most sons, he preferred to believe that everything was fine for their parents, whom they loved but sometimes found annoying. [2918] \"Yes, sir, it's a very pleasant place. I went there on holiday last year. My wife loved it.\" [2919] \"I'm glad to hear that. As for the newspapers, we want\u2014\"\n[2920] \"I heard you have a play on in London, sir. Quite delightful, they told me.\" [2921] \"Yes, it's been excellent.\" [2922] \"I heard it's called 'The Elephant Really Forgets.' Please forgive me for asking, sir, but I always thought elephants wouldn't\u2014my meaning is, forget things.\" [2923] \"Yes, yes, you're right. I already thought it was a mistake to name it that. Many people have told me so.\" [2924] \"It's a fact of natural history that I have always believed.\" [2925] \"Yes, yes. Like all the good moms are beetles.\" [2926] \"Really, sir? Oh, I had no idea about that.\" [2927] \"Regarding the newspaper \u2013\"\n[2928] \"The Times, I believe?\" Mr. Tottman picked up his pencil and stopped midway. [2929] \"The Worker's Daily.\" Edmund spoke firmly. [2930] \"And The Daily Telegraph.\" Phyllis said. [2931] \"And The New Statesman.\" Edmund said. [2932] \"Radio Times.\" Philippa said. [2933] \"And The Spectator.\" Edmund said. [2934] \"And The Gardener's Gazette.\" Philippa said. [2935] Both of them stopped to catch their breath. [2936] \"Thank you, sir,\" Mr. Toothman said. \"I suppose there's The Daily Telegraph?\" [2937] \"No.\" Edmund said. [2938] \"No.\" Phyllida said. [2939] \"Forgive me, you really don't want The Daily Telegraph?\" [2940] \"No.\" [2941] \"No.\" [2942] \"You mean,\" Mr. Tottman liked to have things clear, \"you really don't want The Times!\" [2943] \"Yes, we don't.\" [2944] \"Certainly not.\" [2945] \"You don't subscribe to The North Bampton News and Chipping Cleghorn Gazette either?\" [2946] \"No.\" [2947] \"You don't want me to bring it for you each week?\" [2948] \"No.\" Edmund added, \"Isn't that clear now?\" [2949] \"Ah, yes, sir, yes.\" [2950] Edmund and Philippa went out, and Mr. Tuttman shuffled into the back sitting room. [2951] \"Do you have a pencil, Mrs. Tottman?\" he asked. \"My pen's worn out.\" [2952] \"Yes,\" Mrs. Tottman grabbed the subscription book, \"I'll write it. What did they subscribe to?\" [2953] \"The Worker's Daily, The Daily Telegraph, The New Statesman, The Radio Age, The Observer\u2014let me think, The Gardener's Gazette.\" [2954] \"The Gardener's Gazette,\" she repeated, busy writing, \"and The Telegram.\" [2955] \"They don't want The Telegram.\" [2956] \"Why not?\" [2957] \"They don't want The News. That's what they said.\" [2958] \"Nonsense,\" said Mrs. Tuttman, \"You must not have heard them clearly. They certainly want The News! Everyone subscribes to The News, otherwise how would they know what's going on around them?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAnd this long-handled deep frying pan, this pan, I only use it to boil milk\u2014\"\n[2618] \"Well, I don't know which pan you use for what,\" Julia said angrily, \"you should go to bed, why are you getting up again? I can't even imagine. Get out of my way, let me wash the dishes in peace.\" [2619] \"No, you can't use my kitchen.\" [2620] \"Oh, Midge, you're unbearable!\" [2621] Julia angrily stormed out of the kitchen just as the doorbell rang. [2622] \"I'm not going to answer the door!\" Micky called from the kitchen. Julia muttered a European swear word and strode to the front door. [2623] It was Miss Quincannon. [2624] \"Good evening,\" she croaked, \"I'm sorry to barge in again. I assume the inspector called, right?\" [2625] \"He didn't tell us you were coming,\" Julia said, leading the guest into the living room. [2626] \"He said I didn't have to come unless I wanted to,\" Miss Chicliff said. \"But I very much wanted to.\" [2627] No one offered Miss Chicliff any sympathy for her situation or mentioned Miss Muggleton's death. The tall, robust woman, with a look of relief on her face, made any words of pity seem inadequate. [2628] \"Turn all the lights on,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Put some more coal on the fire. I'm so cold\u2014so very cold. Come and sit by the fire, Miss Chicliff. The Inspector said he'd be here in fifteen minutes, and it's about time now.\" [2629] \"Micki's coming down again,\" Julia said. [2630] \"Is she? Sometimes I think the girl is mad\u2014mad as a hatter. But perhaps we're all mad.\" [2631] \"I can't stand the idea of all criminals being mad,\" exclaimed Miss Quincannon indignantly. \"To me, they're all sane\u2014even clever\u2014in a queer way.\" [2632] There was a sound of a car coming up, and presently Colonel Cadorick, with the Captain and Mrs. Sweeney and the Sweeney children, entered. They all looked very grave. Then Colonel Cadorick lowered his voice and said: \"Oh! Oh! It's getting warm in here!\" [2633] Mrs. Cadorick made an effort to liven up the atmosphere, and her efforts were almost grotesque. [2634] \"Terrible, isn't it?\" she said. \"I mean all this. You know, one slip of the tongue and you never know who's going to be next\u2014just like the plague.\" [2635] \"Mummy,\" said Edmund in an agony of patience, \"can't you stop talking?\" [2636] \"I promise, dear, I won't say another word,\" Mrs. Swetnam said, then sat down on the couch next to Julia. Constable Colquhoun stood near the door, facing a row of three women \u2013 Julia and Mrs. Swetnam on the couch, and Mrs. Eustace on the arm of her husband's chair. It was not intentional, but the arrangement worked perfectly. [2637] Miss Blacklock and Miss Quincannon were bent over the fire. Edmund stood nearby, and Philipa was in the shadows at the back. [2638] Constable Colquhoun began bluntly, \"As you all know, Miss Mulgrave was murdered. We have reason to believe that the killer is a woman. For other reasons, we can narrow down the suspect pool. I would like to ask each of you ladies what you were doing from 4:00 PM to 4:20 PM today. I have already heard from the young lady who claims to be Miss Simmons. I would like her to repeat what she said. Meanwhile, Miss Simmons, I must remind you that if you believe your answer could incriminate you, you do not have to answer. Everything you say will be recorded by Detective Edwards.\" [2639] \"Do you really have to say that?\" Julia asked. Her face was exceptionally pale, but her demeanor was calm. \"I repeat, from 4:00 PM to 4:30 PM, I was walking along the field next to the stream leading to the farmhouse at Compton. I walked back to the road through the field with three white poplars. As far as I can remember, I did not encounter anyone. I did not approach the Gravel Pit House.\" [2640] \"Mrs. Swetnam?\" [2641] Edmund asked: \"Is your warning for all of us?\" [2642] The superintendent turned to him. [2643] \"No. At present, it is only Miss Simmons. I have no reason to believe that what anyone else says will incriminate themselves, but anyone, of course, has the right to have a lawyer present and refuse to answer questions when the lawyer is not present.\" [2644] \"Oh, it's so stupid and completely wasted time,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said loudly. \"I swear I can tell you what I was doing at that time. That's what you want, isn't it? Can I start now?\" [2645] \"Yes, please, Mrs. Sweetnam.\" [2646] \"Now let me think.\" Mrs. Swetnam closed her eyes, then opened them again, \"Of course, I have nothing to do with Miss Mulgrew's murder, and I'm sure everyone here knows that. However, I'm a person who understands the ways of the world, and I know that the police have to ask some of the most unnecessary questions and write down the answers very carefully, because it's all for what they call 'records,' isn't it?\" [2647] Mrs. Swetnam suddenly asked this question to Detective Edwards, who was an excellent stenographer but lacked the art of diplomacy. His face turned red to his ears, and he replied, \"No problem, ma'am. Maybe a little slower would be better.\" [2648] Mrs. Swetnam continued her long speech and made clear pauses where she thought commas or periods were appropriate. [2649] \"Of course, it's hard to be exact, because my sense of time isn't very strong. Since the war, half of our clocks don't work at all, and the other half, because they're not wound up, are either fast or slow or don't work at all.\" Mrs. Swetnam stopped, letting everyone absorb this picture of chaos in timekeeping, and then continued sincerely, \"I think I was darning my socks at four o'clock \u2013 because of some unusual circumstances, I had reversed the direction \u2013 using silver and gold thread, you know, not plain white cloth \u2013 but if I hadn't been doing that, I would have been outside trimming the dead flowers \u2013 no, that was earlier, before the rain.\" [2650] \"That rain,\" the Inspector said, \"started at four-fifteen.\" [2651] \"Really? That's helpful. Of course, I was upstairs at the time, with a basin in the passage to catch the rain, which always leaks there, and the rain comes in so fast that I immediately guessed the gutter must be blocked again. So I came down to put on my raincoat and rubber boots. I called Edmund, but he didn't answer, so I thought he must have been writing at the crucial part of his novel, and I didn't bother him. Besides, I often do it myself. You know, with a broom, a broomstick tied to a long stick to push up the window.\" [2652] \"You mean,\" noticed Corcoran, seeing his subordinate's puzzled expression, \"you were cleaning the gutter?\" [2653] \"Yes, it was all blocked up with leaves. It took me a long time, and I got quite wet, but I finally cleared it. Then I went home to change \u2013 the smell of the dead leaves was awful. Then I went to the kitchen, put the kettle on the fire.\" [2654] Detective Edwards blinked. [2655] \"That means,\" Mrs. Swetnam concluded triumphantly, \"that the actual time was twenty to five.\" [2656] \"Or very close,\" she added. [2657] \"Did anyone see you outside cleaning the gutter?\" [2658] \"No, not at all,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said. \"If there had been anyone, I would have called him to help! It's so hard to do it alone.\" [2659] \"So, according to your statement, you were outside in the rain wearing a raincoat and galoshes, and you were cleaning the gutter, but you have no one to vouch for that?\" [2660] \"You can see the gutter for yourself,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said. \"It's spotless.\" [2661] \"Did you hear your mother call you, Mr. Swetnam?\" [2662] \"No,\" Edmund answered. \"I was sleeping soundly.\" [2663] \"Edmund,\" his mother chided. \"I thought you were writing.\" [2664] Inspector Corrado turned to Mrs. Eastbrook: \"Your turn, Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [2665] \"I was sitting in Arch's study with him,\" Mrs. Eastbrook answered, looking at him with innocent eyes. \"We were listening to the radio, right, Arch?\" [2666] There was a brief pause. Colonel Eastbrook blushed and took his wife's hand. [2667] \"You don't understand these things, kitty,\" he said. \"I\u2014well\u2014I must say, Inspector, you've taken us quite by surprise. My wife, you know, has been very upset by all this. She's on edge, very tense, and she doesn't understand the importance of\u2014considering\u2014before making a statement.\" [2668] \"Archibald,\" Mrs. Eastbrook reprimanded him. \"Are you going to say you weren't with me?\" [2669] \"No, right? I mean, one must be truthful. It's very important in these interrogations. I was talking to Mr. Lumpson, the farmer from Crofton, about how to make money from chickens. It was a quarter to four. I came home just as the rain stopped, just before tea, at a quarter to five. Laura was baking scones.\" [2670] \"So you were out, too, Mrs. Eastbrook?\" [2671] Her pretty face became more and more like a weasel's, her eyes had a trapped look. [2672] \"No - no, I was just sitting and listening to the radio, I didn't go out. Not then. I went out earlier, about - about half past three, just for a little walk, not far.\" [2673] She looked expectant for more questions, but Corradini said calmly, \"That's all, Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [2674] He continued, \"The statement will be typed out. You can look it over, and if it's correct, please sign it.\" [2675] Mrs. Eastbrook glared at him suddenly. [2676] \"Why don't you ask others where they were? Like that woman, Hammers? Edmund Sweeney? How do you know he was really sleeping in the house? No one saw him.\" [2677] Chief Inspector Corrado said calmly, \"Miss McGarrett said some things before she died. On the night of the robbery that happened in this house, someone wasn't here at the time. Miss McGarrett told her friend the names of the people she saw in the room. By eliminating one by one, she found that there was one person she didn't see.\" [2678] \"No one could see anything,\" Julia said. [2679] \"Miss McGarrett could,\" Miss Chilcote suddenly said in a deep voice, \"she was behind the door, right where Chief Inspector Corrado is standing now. She was the only one who saw everything that happened.\" [2680] \"Ah-ha! That's your imagination, isn't it?\" Mickey asked. [2681] She dramatically entered, slamming the door and pushing Coolidge aside, unusually excited. [2682] \"Oh, you don't let Mickey come in with others, do you, you stiff policeman! I'm just Mickey, the kitchen's Mickey! Let her stay in the kitchen! She belongs to the kitchen! But I'll tell you, Mickey sees as well as others, maybe even clearer. Yes, I saw something on the night of the robbery, and I'm convinced of it, and I haven't said it until now. I thought, I won't tell what I saw until the time is right, I'll wait.\" [2683] \"So you're going to ask someone for some money, huh?\" Coolidge said. [2684] Mickey turned to him, looking like an angry cat. [2685] \"Why not? Why do you look down on people? Since I've been so generous in keeping silent, why shouldn't I get paid? Especially when there's going to be money - a lot of money. Ah! I heard - I know what's going on. I know this 'Pip Emma' - this one -\" she suddenly pointed a finger at Julia, \"the secret society that's acting as a spy inside.\" Yes, I could have waited for the money - but now I'm afraid. I'd rather be safe. Because maybe soon someone will kill me. So I'm going to say what I know.\" [2686] \"Well, okay,\" the Inspector said suspiciously, \"what do you know?\" [2687] \"I'll tell you,\" Midge said solemnly, \"that night I wasn't in the silverware room cleaning silver, when I heard the gunshot, I was already in the dining room. I peered through the keyhole, the hallway was dark, but the gunshot was loud, and the flashlight fell to the ground - I saw her. I saw her with a gun in her hand, right next to him. I saw Miss Blacklock.\" [2688] \"Me?\" Miss Blacklock jumped up from her seat, \"you must be crazy!\" [2689] \"But that's impossible,\" Edmund called out, \"Midge couldn't have seen Miss Blacklock.\" [2690] Coladock suddenly interrupted him, his voice acidic and sarcastic. [2691] \"Why not? Just because the woman holding the gun wasn't Miss Blacklock? So it was you, wasn't it?\" [2692] \"I \u2013 certainly not \u2013 damn it \u2013\"\n[2693] \"It was you who stole Colonel Eastbrook's revolver. It was you who conspired with Rudi Schlesinger \u2013 to pull off a joke. You followed Patrick Simmons into the little drawing room, and when the lights went out, you slipped out through the oiled door. You shot Miss Blacklock, and then you killed Rudi Schlesinger. A few seconds later, you came back into the drawing room, striking a match.\" [2694] For a moment, Edmund seemed at a loss for words, then he blurted out, \"The whole idea is too horrible to contemplate. Why me? What motive could I have?\" [2695] \"If Miss Blacklock had died before Mrs. Goodale, remember, there would have been two people to inherit the legacy. We only know them as Pip and Emma. Julia Simmons was really Emma \u2013\"\n[2696] \"And you think I'm Pip?\" Edmund laughed heartily. \"What a ridiculous idea \u2014 utterly ridiculous! I'm just about the right age \u2014 that's all. I can prove to you, you damned fool, that I'm Edmund Swetnam. Birth certificate, school certificates, university degrees \u2014 everything.\" [2697] \"He's not Pip.\" A voice came from the shadows in the corner. Philippa Hemmings stepped forward, pale-faced. \"I am Pip, Inspector.\" [2698] \"You, Mrs. Hemmings?\" [2699] \"Yes. Everyone seems to think Pip is a boy \u2014 of course, Julia knew her twin was a girl, but I didn't know why she didn't say this afternoon \u2014\"\n[2700] \"For family harmony,\" Julia said. \"I suddenly realized who you were. But up until then, I didn't know.\" [2701] \"I agree with Julia,\" Philippa said, her voice trembling slightly. \"After losing my husband and the end of the war, I didn't know what to do. My mother died many years ago. I discovered our Goodel family's relatives. Mrs. Goodel was dying, and when she passed away, the money would fall into the hands of Miss Blacklock. I found out where Miss Blacklock lived, so I\u2014I came here. I found a job at Mrs. Lucas' house. I hoped, since Miss Blacklock was an old lady and had no relatives, she might be willing to help me. Not for me\u2014because I can work\u2014but to help Harry's education. After all, it's Goodel family money, and she has no particular relatives to spend it on.\" [2702] \"Later,\" Philippa spoke faster, as if the floodgates of long-held emotions had suddenly opened, \"after the robbery, I began to feel afraid. Because it seemed to me that the only person who could have a motive to kill Miss Blacklock was me. I had no idea which one was Emma\u2014we're not identical twins, and we don't look alike at all. So, it seemed that I was the only one who should be suspected.\" [2703] She paused, brushing her hair back from her face. Coleridge suddenly realized that the faded snapshot in the letterbox must be Philippa's mother. The resemblance was undeniable. He also understood why the phrase \"hands repeatedly clenching and unclenching\" sounded so familiar\u2014Philippa was doing it right now. [2704] \"Miss Blacklock treated me very well, very well\u2014I never intended to kill her, and I never had such a thought. But it's still the same, I am Pip.\" She added, \"You see, you don't have to suspect Edmund anymore.\" [2705] \"No need?\" Coleridge said, his voice again with that biting tone. \"Edmund Swetnam is a man who loves money. A young man, perhaps wanting to marry a rich woman. But if Miss Blacklock didn't die before Mrs. Goodel, the woman he wanted to marry wouldn't be rich. Since it's almost certain that Mrs. Goodel will die before Miss Blacklock, Swetnam, isn't it, has to do something, isn't it?\" [2706] \"This is all damned lies!\" Edmund shouted. [2707] Just then, there was a sudden cry, coming from the kitchen - a long, terrifying scream of fear. [2708] \"It's not Midge!\" Julia exclaimed. [2709] \"No,\" Inspector Corrado said, \"it's the killer who murdered three people\u2026\"\n[2710] Chapter 22: The Truth Revealed\n[2711] As the inspector turned his attention to Edmund Sweeney, Midge slipped out of the living room and back into the kitchen. She was filling the sink with water when Miss Blacklock suddenly walked in. [2712] Midge felt too ashamed to look her in the eye. [2713] \"You really know how to lie, Midge,\" Miss Blacklock said cheerfully. \"Here \u2013 dishes aren't washed like this. First, you fill the sink with water. You can't wash anything with just two inches of water.\" [2714] Midge obediently turned on the faucet again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."